				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 27)
				{
					text2 = "Monday demonstrations";
					text = "A series of mass protests by citizens of a peaceful nature began. The first demonstration took place in Leipzig, and was repeated every evening on Monday, and demonstrations began to be held in other major cities of the GDR. Dresden, Halle, Karl-Marx-Stadt, Magdeburg, Plauen, Arnstadt, Rostock, Potsdam and Schwerin joined the movement. The troops in the cities in order to avoid bloodshed were left in the barracks. The Stasi suggests that we close the churches by any means, where, they say, these free-thinking ideas spreading and all plans are being prepared, but we see that the unrest is spontaneous. Maybe we should just arrest the organizers? Or try to give water cannons to the soldiers, to disperse the crowd after the crowd, having introduced an emergency regime in cities with unrest for a period of 3 months? There is also an option to make concessions to protesters... Having democratized the regime - a large-scale easing of censorship, regulation and regulation of free rallies, free discussions in the Party for further reforms and changes, but where will they lead us?...|Or try to make only a semblance of reforms - returning the Constitution of 1949, with national names, lands, a large division of powers and the post of the President. True, if the People's Chamber elects him our General Secretary, then discontent may arise...";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 28)
				{
					text2 = "Neither from the West nor from the East";
					text = "Gunter Mittag played a serious role in shaping the foreign policy of the GDR. As party leader of the economy, Mittag closely interacted with Alexander Shalk-Golodkovsky, was dedicated to covert monetary and financial operations, oversaw the unspoken economic contacts with FRG, played an important role in the negotiations for obtaining «German-German» loans. Mittag's connections with Western financiers and politicians aroused dissatisfaction with the orthodox wing of the party elite and the cautiousness of the leadership of the CPSU. Subsequently, Mittag talked about a tough confrontation with the orthodox, figuratively speaking of «ambush shots». Contrary to Brezhnev's dissatisfaction, Honecker sanctioned Mittag's activities, since the GDR was in desperate need of financial assistance from the FRG. However, by the end of the 1980s, Mittag stated that the economy of the GDR could no longer wait for salvation «neither from the west, nor from the east».|Perhaps we should rely on our own resources and further automate the economy? Or is it worth trying to get help from the West by allowing the functioning of small, medium and joint ventures, allowing foreign investments in underdeveloped and unprofitable spheres? However, we can try to attract assistance from the West and from the East and even from the whole planet - by moving to market lines in the economy, opening it for foreign investment in full, and even introducing multiple benefits for foreigners. But who is ready for this in our time?..";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 29)
				{
					text2 = "New forum";
					text = "We want order and not guardianship. We want freedom and confidence for responsible people. We want to be protected from violence, but we should not tolerate dictatorship and espionage... To resolve contradictions, we need to listen to all opinions and arguments, take into account group, and common interests, we need a democratic dialogue on the rule of law, economy and culture.We have something to say to the country on all these issues.With the willingness and desire to do this will depend on whether we can find a way out of the current crisis... As many people as possible participate in the process of public reform. Individual and group proposals should be considered and agreed upon. Together we create a political platform for the entire GDR, for people from all social strata, parties and groups ready to take part in the discussion of the country's problems. For this complex initiative, we choose the name: «New forum.» |There is no talk of a change of power and, even more so, of reunification with FRG, but the party platform of democratic Marxism headed by Comrade Gizi, if he is still a comrade, sends us a collective letter requesting the implementation of reforms to bring people closer and de-bureaucratization of the system.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 31)
				{
					text2 = "Release the hounds";
					text = "The situation in the country is heating up, the people demand reforms, and among the party elite, too, a confusion begins. But the latter have a way out - put the whole blame on Honecker, in order to avoid anger. Yes, and Moscow craves this denouement to take care of such a conservative leader. In the evening, Krenz and Mielke are trying to get support on the phone about the removal of Honecker from the rest of the Politburo members.|Should we unseat Honecker, which, of course, will show the people and the world the coming reforms, but can become the first stone in the avalanche of the fall of the elders from SED and the whole system? However, disgruntled crowds now see Honecker as their first enemy and if we unseat him, we will have to choose:|Will the country lead Comrade Krenz, ready for moderate reforms and dialogue with the opposition, but not wanting to surrender the positions of Leninism in government?|Or the power will be seized by the head of the Stasi, Comrade Mielke, who is ready to appear before " + this.global1.sovietPremiereName + " as an analog of his favorite Andropov. However, his power rule can lead both to market reforms and to the strengthening of socialism, since the siloviks always adhere to the pragmatic positions of today's problems.|We also have a third candidate... The head of the party opposition Gizi, who is ready to immediately democratize society and lead the country on the precepts of democratic socialism, rejecting the ideas of Leninism altogether.";
