					text = "In connection with the introduction of a multiparty system in the GDR, soon there must pass the first free parliamentary elections. A cursory analysis of public opinion by our intelligence shows that the probability of winning by the CDU right party is almost 70%. Such a development of the situation is likely to lead to further reforms, and subsequently to the entry of the GDR into the FRG. If we do not want to allow this, we should consider any of the plans proposed by our agents. The first involves the creation of a coalition from the Party of Democratic Socialism with many left parties and the subsequent support of this bloc by our part. This is a good option, but it will require large cash infusions. The second plan is less expensive and involves the integration of PDS with green. But it is unlikely that such a bloc will get a constitutional majority. And, of course, we can leave things on their own and do not interfere.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 107)
				{
					text2 = "Refugees from the East";
					text = "It seems that Perestroika in the USSR is coming to its logical conclusion. However, the desired increase in welfare did not happen - the socio-economic situation of the people has deteriorated significantly due to inept liberalization of the economy. Soviet citizens start to go abroad massively, seeking stability and confidence in the future. We have not been circumvented by this flow of refugees either, the borders are full with crowds of people, and we should decide how to deal with these people. Please note that if we accept them, we will appear better in the eyes of the people, but to settle all of them will require money, and do we need a huge mass of those who once supported Perestroika? Maybe it's worth taking only a few belonging to our nationality, which will be also well-received by the people, but will be much cheaper? Or is it worth opening a transit to the West, which, it seems, is not against to deal with it by themselves? On the other hand, by adopting them, we can use it in our propaganda, showing the pernicious consequences of the reforms. However, many people still associates the USSR with socialism and denial of Western democracy, so it is possible to show to the whole world the USSR in the blaming light. Grateful for refuge, the refugees seem to support any decision.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 102)
				{
					text2 = "Oil curse";
					text = "It is known that our country has large oil deposits, but almost all the mining complexes are technically obsolete by our days, they do not work efficiently, and the equipment periodically breaks down. Since now we have coped with most of the internal issues, can it be worthwhile to start modernizing the oil towers and bringing them to modern standards? This project will be costly and there will be almost no instantaneous return, as modernization needs time, but it will provide many with work, raise the standard of living and, in the future, will push the development of our economy and strengthen the international position. Perhaps, in order to avoid large momentary costs, we should take credit from Western countries, because only they can now help us. However, this means some credit dependence on the West and that part of the new profits will go to pay off debts. And our country has sad memories of attempts to develop at the expense of external debts. Maybe we should not do anything at all?";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 95)
				{
					text2 = "Anniversary";
					text = "Soon our country will celebrate the 45th anniversary of liberation from fascism, and our president, of course, must make a speech. " + this.global1.politics_name[this.global1.data[11]] + " wants to criticize Perestroika in the USSR, but should we confront? Some party members only suggest that he speak out about the inadmissibility of Perestroika in Romania.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 99)
				{
					text2 = "Bucharest Spring";
					text = "Our country is in chaos and it seemed that nothing could help us, but our intelligence service learned about the USA note addressed to the USSR Ministry of Foreign Affairs, which states that the Americans are not against the introduction of Soviet troops into Romania in order to establish order. Securitate may appeal to " + this.global1.sovietPremiereName + " that the USSR should introduce troops.|Cut from intelligence data: Our ambassador in the USA Silviu Brucan, who recently turned to the opposition of Ceausescu, participated in a secret meeting last month with Mikhail Gorbachev, at which was discussed the question of the possible overthrow of the Romanian leader. As the agent notes: «Gorbachev was very familiar with the situation in Romania. From the very beginning, he said that he agreed with the proposal to eliminate Ceausescu from power, but with the condition of maintaining the leadership role of the RCP in Romanian society. He kept repeating this phrase almost mechanically, warning that in the opposite case, Romania would become chaotic». The United States, even at the beginning of the Revolution in Romania, notified the Soviet Union that: «in connection with the crisis of the Ceausescu regime, they will not oppose the intervention of the Soviet Union and its allies in Romania». This note, according to the memoirs of Anatoly Chernyaev, US Secretary of State James Baker sent to the Soviet Ministry of Foreign Affairs.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 100)
				{
					text2 = "Military affairs";
					text = "Ceausescu is no longer the president of Romania, but angry people on the streets demand that we dismiss the generals, who ordered the shooting of peaceful protesters. Of course, we can fulfill the demands of the people, but some figures of special services offer us to pay miners so that they, armed with armature, will dispersed the protesters.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 106)
				{
					text2 = "Decomunization";
					text = "The old socialist regime fell. Soviet influence is coming to naught and we can benefit from this, especially if we want to integrate in Europe. Now we must raze to the ground any symbol of occupation, any symbol of socialism. We propose to legislatively prohibit any demonstration of the symbolism of the old authorities and large-scale decommunization of our country, returning even to small streets their historical names. Of course, there will be dissatisfied, but the West and the liberals, including the opposition, will appreciate this.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 103)
				{
					text2 = "King-Komsomolets";
					text = "Today, the former Romanian king Mihai First himself addressed us. Having learned about the liquidation of the Ceausescu regime, he asks permission to visit his homeland. Recall that Mihai is obviously a popular figure in our country, he saved it from total defeat in the Second World War, when he removed Antonescu and refused to conduct further military operations, for which he was awarded the Order of Victory. After the liquidation of the monarchy, he emigrated to Switzerland and is going to return to us now. This will unite the people and raise its spirit, but the former king can unite not only the people, but also the opposition.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 96)
				{
					text2 = "XIV Congress of the RCP";
					text = "Soon the 14th Congress of the RCP should take place, which should determine the fate of our country. The people of Romania, amazed by what is happening in the socialist camp, are waiting for change and we can not ignore it. Ceausescu can approve Manea Manescu as an interim president to cool the people's ardor, and in 5 years appoint his son as president. But which one? Ceausescu has long promoted his son, Nicu, who once led the Young Communist League of Romania, however, despite Ceausescu's attempts to appoint him mentors, Nicu remains capricious and rash person. On the other hand, we have Valentin, but despite all attempts to introduce him into politics, he remains a closed person, aimed primarily at work on scientific activities.";
					if (this.global1.data[56] <= 0)
					{
						text += "|However, due to the unstable situation in the country, there is a conspiracy of radical leftists unsatisfied with the state of affairs in the state, so that the fate of Ceausescu himself remains vague.";
					}
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 93)
				{
					text2 = "Letter of Six";
					text = "Today the Stalinist Gheorghe Apostol, a party activist who had previously actively criticized the policy of Ceausescu, together with the other 5 veterans of the RCP and dissidents, wrote an open letter to the current president, in which he accused Ceausescu of violating human rights in Romania. Later the letter was read out on \"Radio Liberty\" and \"Voice of America\". Conservative party members propose not to stand on ceremony with the enemy of the people and expose him and other dissidents to capital punishment. However, more moderate figures suggest that the Apostol be placed under house arrest, so as not to provoke the people.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 94)
				{
					text2 = "Austerity";
					text = "As you know, for the prompt payment of external debts, a special austerity mode has been introduced in our country, because of which our population is experiencing difficulties even with essential goods. In today's report of the Minister of Finance, it is said about the full payment of Romania's foreign debt, which can not but rejoice. However, our reserve is empty and some party leaders propose not to abolish the austerity or at least to do it gradually.|Remember, this decision will be the solution of the entire upcoming Five-Year Plan, and therefore can not be canceled until its end!";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 110)
				{
					text2 = "The final decision of the Hungarian issue";
					text = "The nationalism that has been observed throughout Ceausescu's reign and which has been especially aggravated recently has led to clashes between the Romanians and the Hungarian minority in Transylvania, long oppressed, due to the rhetoric and actions of Ceausescu. Tired of the oppression Hungarians began to organize demonstrations and organize themselves to fight against aggressive Romanians. We urgently need to do something before we share the fate of neighboring Yugoslavia. We can continue the old line, disperse the Hungarian meetings and turn a blind eye to the actions of the Romanian nationalists, watching only the order in the region, but how long can such a policy still keep the country from splitting up? Maybe we should start suppressing any nationalism by the forces of Securitate and the police, propagandizing the friendship of the peoples? Part of the population and nationalists in the party will remain unhappy, but this will allow us to soften the national question. Or you can go further, reviving the Magyar Autonomous Region abolished in 1968. Or let the Hungarians leave for Hungary, because we are the only country in the socialist camp where, after World War II, a large national minority left. But we will lose workers and minds, and to release the oppressed minority to unstable Hungary may not be the best idea.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 92)
				{
					text2 = "The German miracle";
					text = "In the GDR there is a congress of the ruling party of the SED. Today we learned from the news that General Secretary Erich Honecker, not without the assistance of the Stasi, routed the anti-Party defeatist group Krenz-Mittag, who intended to move to market reforms and displace Honecker himself, after which they and their supporters were dismissed and expelled from party. Perestroika in the GDR failed and the current ruler announced the Congress decision on the new five-year plan, with an emphasis on large-scale automation of the economy of the lower and middle levels, using the latest technologies. This congress and its decisions have already provoked criticism in Moscow. But is it worth repeating after the USSR? And will not we try to switch to automation? ";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 97)
				{
					text2 = "RESHUFFLES IN THE APPARATUS";
					text = string.Concat(new string[]
					{
						"Recently, a wave of unrest broke out across Romanian cities, after which the General Secretary of the Central Committee of the RCP, Nicolae Ceausescu, expressed his displeasure to the Minister of Defense Vasile Milea, the head of Securitate General Iulian Vlad and the Minister of Internal Affairs Tudor Postelnicu with the activities of punitive agencies and said that he suspected them of betrayal. Not being satisfied with their allegations of personal devotion to him, Ceausescu announced the removal of all three from their posts, but ",
						this.global1.politics_name[this.global1.data[12]],
						" stick up for them. Now Ceausescu needs to decide how to act in this situation. Given the difficult situation, it may be worthwhile to demonstrate strength and hardness by removing also ",
						this.global1.politics_name[this.global1.data[12]],
						" or, refusing from this, threats to force the army and Securitate to act stiffer. Or maybe it is not necessary to aggravate the situation and just listen to the Prime Minister's words and refuse removal?"
					});
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 98)
				{
					text2 = "Revolution in Romania";
					text = "The spread of propaganda of the Western media, as well as the apparent dissatisfaction part of the popular masses with government's policy, led to a general tense in the country. At the moment, the situation is out of control - in Timisoara, the ethnic Hungarian minority began to rage after the arrest of the dissident priest (Hungarian), there were several provocations by the illegal Hungarian pan-nationalists, which forced to involved the troops of the Ministry of Internal Affairs. The Western media launched a campaign against Ceausescu, showing on the air photos of the \"corpses of the victims dead from shooting police and the army of Romania\". Their authenticity is rather controversial, but given the emotional background, the Romanian population (its active part) will most likely believe the most outright lies of foreign media than the most truthful reportage of Romanian ones. Protest actions are organized near the Romanian embassies in some countries, and in Bucharest groups of people who are dissatisfied with the current policy are starting to gather, the main driving force are the students. The slogans against the \"cruelty of the regime\" resonate with frank calls for the overthrow of the current government. The situation has become so heated that it is not known how flexible the army leadership will be. The phones of the Soviet Embassy are not responding. In these circumstances, it is necessary to act decisively to prevent the fall of Romania as a state and Ceausescu took the initiative...";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 101)
				{
					text2 = "Revenge of the tyrant?";
					text = "The vengeance and trial of Ceausescu certainly aroused excitement and even split the Romanian society. Georgica Popa, without enthusiasm, condemned couple of former rulers and asked to go abroad.. However, soon we heard rumors that one of the Ceausescu lawyers was found dead, and some involved in the process were hospitalized in serious condition. Georgica, despite the fact that he received a secured apartment, was found dead today. Judging by the suicide note, it's a suicide, but... What if someone took revenge on judge from Ceausescu's supporters? Or did someone take him away as an unnecessary witness? We should take action, otherwise there will be rumors that can spread panic. ";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 105)
				{
					text2 = "Intensity of emotions";
					text = "Urgent news! Resolutions of Western countries with the support of the NATO bloc convinced the IAEA commission to conduct a check for the presence of nuclear weapons, referring to our «aggressive policy». I remind you that our country as a participant of the NPT has obligations to the IAEA commission. So what do we do? Of course, we can comply with our agreements and allow foreign inspectors, but they will certainly find nuclear weapons in our territory. But maybe it's worth using our agent network and conducting a commission on a false trace? This is a very dangerous undertaking, but if it succeed, we will manage to get away with it. But some party members suggest simply not to let the commission into our territory, and more radical ones say that we do not need to stand on ceremony with them and recognize us as a nuclear power. ";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 111)
				{
					text2 = "Independent Society";
					text = "Recently, the Bulgarian party press launched an aggressive campaign to promote the Independent Society for the Protection of Human Rights, a dissident association created with the help of the 5th KGB Directorate of the USSR, at the initiative of human rights activist Ilya Minev, a 70-year-old nationalist dissident, an anti-communist, 33 years spent in DS prisons. The society advocated the protection of human rights in Bulgaria, the restoration of the dignity of Bulgarian citizens. First of all, it was about the victims of political persecution, prisoners and national minorities. Also, the Society advocated the democratization of Bulgaria, the conduct of political and economic reforms, independence from the USSR and leave from both the Warsaw Pact and CMEA. On December 19, 1986, the leaders of the future association read a document urging the USA and its allies not to finish the work while human rights abuses are being committed in the countries of the socialist camp. This document was later transferred to the US Embassy in Sofia. On October 26, 1988, the Minister of the Interior of Bulgaria, General Stoyanov, submitted a memorandum on the Society and its organizers to Prime Minister Georgi Atanasov. Ilya Minev and his supporters were characterized as convinced and dangerous opponents of the regime. In the winter of 1988, they were arrested and isolated. However, thanks to the party press, they became popular, and there are even assumptions that this campaign is part of the plan of the nomenclature opposition and, possibly, even the Soviet leadership and " + this.global1.sovietPremiereName + " personally to remove us from power. How can we solve this problem?";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 112)
				{
					text2 = "Great excursion";
					text = "Yesterday, in the regions where the majority of the population is ethnic Turks, mass protests, rallies and riots began. The Turks oppose forced Bulgarianization, that is, the replacement of their Turkish names and surnames with Bulgarian ones, as well as against the abolition of learning in Turkish in schools and universities located in their regions. They also demand the democratization of the political system. The state security agencies have a version that the protesters are financed by the West, and in particular by Turkey. By the way, the Soviet Union did not react to these protests. What measures will we take in order to calm the Turks and stabilize the situation in the country? The Minister of the Interior proposes to arrange for them a 'Big Excursion': at night agents of the DS will come to the Turks home and demand to gather their things and go to Turkey in the morning if they do not want to be in the internment camps.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 113)
				{
					text2 = "Balkan revenge";
					text = "Almost 80 years have passed since Bulgaria tried to assert its dominance in the Balkans. During the war Greece, Romania, Turkey, Serbia and Montenegro acted as a united front on the peninsula against it. Bulgaria was defeated, and its border territories are divided among the winners. Attempts to take revenge dragged the country into world wars on the side of Germany, but they also failed. But now, after the death of Tito, Yugoslavia is falling apart. This is an ideal chance to take revenge and return Macedonia to our power. A group of radical party members proposes to recognize the Macedonians as Bulgarians and to introduce troops to Macedonia to protect them. Although this will be an excellent step to give the people a small but victorious war, on the other hand, it is risky and costly, and maybe we should forget the enmity and offer Milošević military assistance, having received an official permission for quartering troops in Macedonia? Truth then, we never get it.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 114)
				{
					text2 = "Sofia's Autumn";
					text = "Right now in Sofia there is a large-scale demonstration. Several thousand people gathered in the square in front of the Bulgarian parliament building. They demand that attention be paid to the state of the environment. As far as we know, the demonstration was organized by the club «Ecoglasnost» in support of glasnost and perestroika, an environmental non-governmental organization created by the Bulgarian scientist Hristo Smolenov and actor Peter Slabakov. Earlier, 20 members of the \"Ecoglasnost\" were arrested by our special services. The public noticeably increased discontent with our authoritarian measures to retain power, plus political and economic pressure from the side of the Soviet Union begins to increase. In the country, the probability of a «velvet revolution» is growing every day. If we use force, radical nationalists and the remaining ethnic Turks will rise along with «Ecoglasnost», together with this the pressure from the USSR will become even greater. Some party members propose to enter into negotiations with the moderate opposition and begin in the country ecological and moderate political reforms in order to appease the protesters. But won't this step be perceived as our weakness, will it?..";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 116)
				{
					text2 = "Relic of stagnation";
					text = "Todor Zhivkov, who ruled Bulgaria for many years, was suspended, we continue to lose trade ties with the former socialist camp, and our economy is experiencing unprecedented difficulties. Reformist forces in the party are proposing to launch market-oriented economic reforms. In their opinion, this will not only help us survive the economic crisis, but will also raise our prestige on the world stage. Naturally, there are several plans for reform. The first, on which the most radical reformers insist, involves the transition to a market economy in the shortest possible time. The second implies the gradual introduction of market elements in the country: artels and cooperatives, foreign investment in the light industry, as well as permission for private farms. In addition, some party members suggest the release of former underground businessman Georgy Naydenov from prison and involve him in the formation of a new economic agenda. They argue this by the fact that once the business of George filled the treasury of our country, so he will be able to raise our economy. But is it worth it to break the system that has been built for years?..";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 117)
				{
					text2 = "Prophecy";
					text = "Recently we received a letter written on behalf of the assistants of the famous prophetess Vanga. In the letter she predicts that if our bright transformations in the country go too far, then we will all be swept away by the blue and white power. On the one hand, it can affect our reputation very painfully, and on the other - it can make our country even freer in terms of freedom of speech. So, what will we do?";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 119)
				{
					text2 = "Cultural heritage";
					text = "In preparation for the celebration of the 1300th anniversary of Bulgaria (it was celebrated in 1981), in 1977 the Cultural Heritage Foundation, headed by the daughter of the General Secretary Lyudmila Zhivkova, was established. Special funds provided for large-scale purchases of cultural property abroad. These operations required Mircho Spasov's indispensable sanction as a functionary of the Central Committee. In this regard, some party members propose to blame Zhivkov and former KGB head Mircho Spasov for organizing corruption schemes, and to expel Spasov from the party and bring him to trial. However, there is an opinion in the party about the need to put not only Spasov, but to launch mass anti-corruption purges throughout the party. However, all this is a good chance to enrich our treasury, because Spasov has accumulated a lot of money, some of which he can transfer to us in exchange for free leaving the country.";
