				else if (this.global1.number_event == 126)
				{
					text2 = "Western threat";
					text = "At the moment, our relations with the United States are deteriorating rapidly. The USA president, through his envoy, tipped us that we should be more friendly towards America, if we do not want any consequences. What should we do?";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 118)
				{
					text2 = "Ancient Bulgarian Tradition";
					text = "Today, our General Secretary, referring to the topic of history in his speech, stated that in the Russo-Turkish and World War II the Bulgarian people were liberated primarily by their own forces, while the support of the Russian Empire and the Soviet Union played a secondary role. This very contradictory statement can give impetus to the strengthening of nationalism in Bulgaria, as well as damage the reputation of our country in the international arena. But it's not too late to fix it, is it?..";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 121)
				{
					text2 = "Great Yugoslavia";
					text = "The idea of Yugoslav irredentism or the creation of the Balkan federation has existed for several decades. Even in pre-war Yugoslavia, monarchists promoted the idea of including Bulgaria, Albania and parts of Greece in the country. Then Josip Broz Tito tried to negotiate with Bulgaria and introduce the military base in Albania, coming into a big conflict with Joseph Stalin. Nevertheless, neither the monarchist's nor Tito's plans were to come true. However, right now in the world there is a geopolitical situation suitable for the implementation of a project similar to Great Yugoslavia, because the Soviet Union is weakening, our troops are stationed in Macedonia, and allied regimes are approved in Albania and Yugoslavia. Maybe it's time to form the \"Balkan Federation\", but with center in Sofia? But how will the world respond to this?..";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 122)
				{
					text2 = "Alyosha";
					text = "The struggle against the communist past is gaining momentum. The rightists demand that you not weaken the pressure and take down the famous monument to the soldier-liberator in Plovdiv, better known as Alyosha. However, the city's residents have not yet realized that they have freed themselves from the red occupation and are massively opposing the demolition of the monument - one of the symbols of the city. To it from all over the country are gathered dissatisfied with the new power and struggle with the past. And some veterans even threaten self-immolation, but they will not stop us on the road to real independence from the bloody communist past?";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 108)
				{
					text2 = "Fall of the Curtain?";
					text = "After the wave of liberalization in the socialist camp, we were invited to change the migration policy and open the country's borders to neighboring countries. On the one hand, this will create a good image for us and, perhaps, will cool the heads of those who believe that abroad is a paradise. But on the other hand, our detractors can take advantage of this. Can we open them only for our allies? And perhaps, it is worthwhile not to open borders at all and to strengthen them to prevent any outside influence? Or we will choose a compromise and open the border for those who can pay? ";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 115)
				{
					text2 = "Reshuffles";
					text = "During his reign in Bulgaria in 1956, Todor Zhivkov concentrated in his hands a huge power and formed even a soft resemblance to the cult of personality. All this is not pleasant to some people in the party top and parliament. Recently, the chairman of the People's Assembly, Stanko Todorov, sent a letter to the Politburo in which he pointed to the diminishing role of the parliament, the formalization of its activities and its subordination to the party leadership. Also the strongest blow to Zhivkov's reputation was the letter of Foreign Minister Petar Mladenov, in which he accused the first of isolating Bulgaria from the rest of the world and even from the USSR, and likened our regime to the regime of Nicolae Ceausescu. Despite the stability in the country and the loyalty of the population, a reformist opposition led by Petar Mladenov and Andrey Lukanov is formed in the party with the support of the USSR and they are ready to make a move by initiating Zhivkov's resignation. It is necessary to decide how to retain the power to the latter (and whether he will be able to retain it at all) - by force or polemic in the Politburo. However, Mladenov is not over-popular, especially among conservatives. In this case, it may be worth remembering about Angel Solakov - the former chairman of the DS, who had a huge influence before the removal of Zhivkov and feared for his power. He is a supporter of national Bulgarian socialism and an uncompromising struggle against anti-state elements and is ready to lead Bulgaria to the future by a firm hand.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 127)
				{
					text2 = "Rehabilitation of Rehabilitation";
					text = "In 1965, a group of conspirators led by Ivan Todorov-Gorunia planned to overthrow Todor Zhivkov because the latter was pursuing a policy that was too revisionist in their opinion. However, the conspiracy was uncovered by law enforcement officers, the conspirators were arrested and convicted, and Ivan himself committed suicide after learning of the disclosure of the plot. Todor Zhivkov is no longer in power, and in connection with this, Todorov-Gorunia's countrymen are demanding to rehabilitate him. We can certainly go for it and rehabilitate him as a victim of Zhivkov's regime, but Ivan was an ardent Stalinist ... Do you see?";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 128)
				{
					text2 = "Battle of special services";
					text = "Recent events related to the opposition growth of reformist ideas in the party and the people, despite the fact that our country has been stable for a long time, have pushed analysts of special services and party members to the idea that this opposition has an artificial external origin. Most likely in our territory, on the orders of Gorbachev, there is a network of KGB agents trying to destabilize the country and push it onto the path of Perestroika. Having followed the sources and the members of the Society, we probably will be able to uncover this network, however, it is necessary to decide how to do it. It is possible to deploy propaganda by showing the true side of Gorbachev's promises that it will raise our popularity among the people and simplify the work of the special services, as all the people will rise up against the spies, but this means directly to go against the USSR. You can quietly catch agents and expel them to the USSR, taking care that they do not deploy the network anew, which will not give such a powerful propaganda effect, but will allow Gorbachev to convey our discontent without direct confrontation. However, the more moderate party members fear both direct and hidden confrontation with Gorbachev and suggest that they call for an honest conversation, show our democracy, agree on holding elections and ask in return to remove the agency support for the opposition. We will have to limit the activities of other parties to a limited extent, and even the existing opposition will remain, although without the strong support, but if we are lucky, then we can avoid quarrels with the USSR and waste of our forces...\n";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 124)
				{
					text2 = "It would be better if tanks have come";
					text = "Tension in the country resulted in terrible events! Because of the leakage of audio recording, where the voice of the current president, Petar Mladenov, states about the protests of the opposition of Zhivkov's times: \"It would be better if tanks have come\", the unrest began. It is not known how such a record has leaked to the people and whether individual party factions or even the KGB are involved in it, but at the moment, the organization of opposition rallies begins - \"Union of Democratic Forces\" and \"Movement for Rights and Freedoms\" they are quite strong, they rely on the stupid \"Western\" propaganda and dissatisfied with the current state of Bulgaria. Given their \"democratic\" orientation, it's easy to guess that in the event of their almost inevitable victory, the Communist Party secretary, Petar Mladenov, will also be forced to resign under the pressure of irreconcilable opponents! It is necessary to act urgently, while this is still possible.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 129)
				{
					text2 = "Hungarian crossroad";
					text = "Comrade General Secretary! As you know, we managed to create our military alliance even stronger than the past, but, without the Hungarian People's Republic, which declared its neutrality. Alas, it was it who had to connect us with our allies and without its membership, our defense is not as effective as it could be. Maybe we should put pressure on the Hungarian government to eliminate this gap?";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 130)
				{
					text2 = "Red rising sun";
					text = "A new issue is on the agenda. Japan is about to hold elections for the upper house of parliament, the House of Councillors. In recent years, the JCP has been steadily gaining in popularity, while remaining a second-rate party. The orthodox part of the party proposes to support them in the electoral struggle by allocating a certain amount of money for this purpose.| In their opinion, if they get a parliamentary majority, we would be able to strengthen the position of Far Eastern socialism and start a more active dialog with Japan. Moreover, the Japanese Communist Party favors the withdrawal of U.S. military bases from the country, which could again play into our hands. On the other hand, the JCP has always kept a low profile, and the current chairman of the Central Committee Presidium, Kenji Miyamoto, has repeatedly criticized the USSR and Leninism in general. In addition, they are in favor of the complete transfer of the Kuril Islands to Japan. For this reason, many in our Party are very skeptical of the idea of further deepening cooperation with the revisionist JCP.";
					if (this.global1.allcountries[44].Donat)
					{
						text += " As an alternative to support for the Japanese Communists, some party members propose to help a more pro-Soviet party, the Japan Socialist Party. It has strong support in the labor unions, favors pacifism in foreign policy, and mending relations with socialist camp countries. Although the party has been deteriorating its results for several election cycles, which has brought it to the brink of splitting between different wings, such a gesture could gain support from the USSR. Moreover, it has always been perceived by Japanese society as the main opposition to the ruling Liberal Democrats, and this is unlikely to change now. Alternatively, support for Komeito, a center-left party closely aligned with the Socialists, which may have a better chance of chipping away some of the electorate from the ruling Liberal Democrats, is also being considered.";
					}
					text += "|Either way, the choice is now ours.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 131)
				{
					text2 = "Return of the old eagle";
					text = "In 1949, Remer founded the Socialist Reich Party, which very quickly turned into a mass neo-Nazi organization, in every way dragging Bonn's relations with the Western alliance through the mud. The former Nazi, who fought on the Eastern Front and suppressed the 1944 conspiracy in Germany, wholeheartedly advocated the alliance of Germany and Russia, against «Anglo-American imperialism and Zionism». «Reich Socialists» could become the third strongest party in Germany, but the newspaper campaign was launched against the Party, and Remer was taken into custody, after which he continued to live abroad.| \nDear comrade ruler! As far as you know, in the early 1980s, Otto Ernst Remer returned to Germany, where he tried to revive the activities of his neo-Nazi party, once funded by the USSR MGB. According to the reports of our agents, he is not doing well, and the German special services are already preparing his neutralization. Maybe we should take advantage of this...?";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 132)
				{
					text2 = "Foundation";
					text = "The faction of \"Polish Concrete\", ardent Stalinists and conservative communists, almost completely lost their influence in the party and society. A significant role in the fall of the faction was played by one of its key figures Albin Siwak, who in the early 1980s called for the suppression of 'Solidarity' and subsequently became one of the main objects of opposition hatred. Given our policy of liberalization, as well as pro-Western sentiment among the people, some party members from the right wing of the party are proposing to displace \"Polish Concrete\" from politics, thus showing our readiness for change. However, maybe we'll leave them, as a safety cushion, in the most extreme case?..";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 133)
				{
					text2 = "Preparation";
					text = "Soon the planned negotiations with the opposition will take place, designed to pave the way for future large-scale reforms in Poland. However, should we go to negotiations without getting ready? The head of the special services suggests organizing an accident for opposition leaders so that we can seize the initiative by using confusion in the ranks of opponents. After all, you can not trust the opposition... But can't not to trust too. ";
					if (this.global1.data[56] < 2)
					{
						text += "|However, the figures of 'party concrete' offer a different plan. Conservatives suggest using special services to return our influence to the Polish Catholic Church. Earlier, the church tried to mediate between the opposition and the state, but now the sympathies of the priests are completely owned by the opposition. According to the figures of the 'concrete', we must resort to bribery, blackmail and intimidation against the clergy, in order to exclude the possibility of supporting the opposition.";
					}
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 134)
				{
					text2 = "Round table";
					text = "Well, the long-awaited round table of power and 'Solidarity' began ";
					if (this.global1.data[57] == 0)
					{
						text += "And although a small group of oppositionists refused to negotiate with the 'criminal authorities', on the whole, the opposition is as united as ever and ready to defend its interests.";
					}
					else if (this.global1.data[57] == 2)
					{
						text += "Thanks to our operation to exclude priests from the political game, the opposition movement weakened throughout the country. Nevertheless, the opposition still plays an important role in the country. Perhaps it is worth using discord among our opponents??..";
					}
					else if (this.global1.data[57] == 1)
					{
						text += "And although earlier there were disputes in the opposition over whether it is worth starting negotiations with the 'murderers of peaceful oppositionists', most of 'Solidarity' decided to come to the round table.";
					}
					if (this.global1.data[56] == 1)
					{
						text += " Separately, the leaders of the 'party concrete' expressed. They offer a radical method of combating the enemies of the people - the use of troops for the shooting of all opposition leaders who came to the round table. Naturally, this will cause an aggravation of relations with almost the entire world and instant radicalization of the opposition, but you did not just introduced conservatives in the government?..";
					}
					else
					{
						text += " The opposition demands from us the introduction of the post of the President of Poland and the holding of free parliamentary and presidential elections. The party divided opinions about what we should do. If the most liberal-minded suggest to completely accept the proposal of 'Solidarity' and lead the country into a 'bright democratic future', the more moderate party members insist on a compromise: reserve the presidency for the current leader for several years and introduce quotas for our party in a multi-party parliament.";
					}
					if (this.global1.data[57] == 2)
					{
						text += " However, a small group of party members offers us another option. In the opposition, the strifes are increasing and we can try to agree with that part of 'Solidarity' that does not agree with the ideas of Lech Walesa and stands on the positions of protecting the interests of the working people. Perhaps we should cooperate with them? Yes, we will have to make concessions: to introduce the post of president, to hold free presidential elections and to start the implementation of a syndicalistic socially-oriented economy. But maybe this is our last chance to stabilize the situation in the country?..";
					}
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 135)
				{
					text2 = "Reorientation";
					if (this.global1.data[59] <= 0)
					{
						text = "Soon the promised elections will be held in our country, and although the people are pleased with our consensus with the opposition, our chances of winning are low. Some party members suggest renewing the ideology of our party in order to keep pace with the times and the sympathies of voters. But is it worth it..?";
					}
					else
					{
						text = "Bourgeois elections have not been approved and the people are still uneasy, and the chair beneath us continues to stagger. Some party members propose to keep pace with the times and renew the ideology of our party, which in their opinion will allow us to win popular sympathy and strengthen our power.";
					}
					if (this.global1.data[56] < 2)
					{
						text += "|In addition, 'party concrete' requested audience with us. They expressed the idea of a return to their origins: the beginning of the construction of national Polish socialism with an emphasis on our traditions and history. However, it is unlikely that the other party members will like this...";
					}
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 136)
				{
					text2 = "Catholic question";
					text = "The Church has long been a strong and influential public institution in Poland, and even a support for power. True, not for our... ";
					if (this.global1.data[57] == 2)
					{
						text += "And although the church has not recovered from our blow, everything can change quickly due to the strong religiousness of the population and the influence of the Pope. Maybe we should try to negotiate with priests to strengthen our power";
					}
					else
					{
						text += "Right now the church is strong, as never before. Maybe we should try to agree with the priests to strengthen our power and knock the ground out from under the feet of the opposition? However, there is also a more radical version of reducing the support of the opposition to the church: we can use our special services.";
					}
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 137)
				{
					text2 = "El Presidente";
					text = "Recently, the first alternative parliamentary elections were held in our country, and now, following world trends, the pressure of the opposition and the people, we are establishing a new post in the country - the President of Poland. If we want to retain power in the country, then we must push our man to such an influential post. The main thing is that the people should be satisfied with the democratic election procedure... But the Sejm will elect the president, and will the parliamentarians elect the person we need, this is a serious question.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 138)
				{
					text2 = "Final solution of the economic question";
					text = "The economic situation in Poland is getting worse and worse day by day: we need more and more money to repay the debt, the Soviet Union no longer gives us economic assistance, our economy is in stagnation, and we are denied new loans. Party members divided into several groups, each of which offers its own anti-crisis plan.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 139)
				{
					text2 = "Cabinet Affairs";
					text = "The current government of our country can not carry out its activities: almost all ministers either do not want to fulfill their powers, referring to a complete mess in the ministry, or are so hated by the population that their own subordinates refuse to carry out their orders. In connection with the current situation, many parliamentarians demand the resignation of the Prime Minister. The population is on the brink, more and more demanding reforms, the ever lesser part of the opposition agrees to negotiate with us, and even our own party is drowning in discord.";
					if (!this.global1.event_done[12] && (!this.global1.event_done[46] || (this.global1.event_done[46] && this.global1.allcountries[5].Gosstroy == 9)))
					{
						text += "|In this difficult situation, Romanian President Nicolae Ceausescu offers us help. He claims that he can use his connections to ask Moscow to use special services to help Polish socialism, if, of course, we agree. But do we need now foreign intervention, or can we cope on our own?";
					}
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 140)
				{
					text2 = "German debts";
					text = "Our countries are leaps and bounds to a bright democratic future, or, at least, we should try to at least convince the West of this. In this regard, some liberal-minded party members suggest opening the border with the GDR, where crowds of people are trying to go abroad, while Honecker closes one border after another. Thus, we will provide refugees with free transit to Western countries and will be as peace advocates for the West. However, conservative party members consider this decision too risky and offer to blackmail the GDR with threats of opening borders with the aim of obtaining a loan that is profitable for us. But now is not the time to shake the socialist camp, right?..";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 141)
				{
					text2 = "Honest, very honest";
					if (this.global1.data[17] >= 17 || this.global1.data[14] >= 4)
					{
						text = "Our country takes leaps and bounds towards a democratic future: the economy, authorities and courts are being reformed, and a healthy civil society is growing up in the country. And the hour is not far off when the citizens of our country will demand the last step to introduce Western democracy in our country - to hold the election of the head of state.";
					}
					else
					{
						text = "More and more citizens of Poland are not satisfied with the policy pursued by us. But most of all of course is blamed our leader. And the hour is not far off when dissatisfied people across the country will come out for protests for holding a free election of the head of state.";
					}
					text += " Of course, we can hold honest national elections, but almost all party members suggest at least to attract public servants to the election under the threat of dismissal, and some even suggest falsifying the results of elections.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 142)
				{
					text2 = "Eternal President";
					text = "Given the line recently pursued by our party, some nationalist-minded party members propose to reburial the Polish commander and politician, the General broni and the prime minister of the Polish government in exile Wladyslaw Sikorski in his homeland. Naturally, our decision can be an excellent occasion to establish relations with the West or support Polish nationalism. Moreover, the naturalness of his death is still in doubt...";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 143)
				{
					text2 = "We can into space!";
					text = "In order to show our strength and greatness to the people to give them the hope of believing in us against the background of all the chaos that is happening in the socialist camp, a group of party members proposes to make Poland a space power. Yes, we had an astronaut, flying together with Soviet cosmonauts, but it's time to create your own cosmodrome, our carrier rockets, our satellites and our shuttles. Yes, it will cost our treasury dearly, but after all the loyalty of the people is more important?..";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 144)
				{
					text2 = "Polish Prometheus";
					text = "GKCHP failed, and this means only one - the quick demise of the socialist camp. Perhaps we should begin to build closer ties with Western countries?.. However, there are ideas that are becoming even more popular in the Party than this. Nationalistic ideas.|Maybe we should resume the 'Prometheus' project in order to provide our country with a worthy place in the new world? We remind you, 'Prometheus' is an old project for dismembering the USSR into national republics, and subsequently joining these countries to the East Slavic Federation under the leadership of Great Poland. But is it worth it?..";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 145)
				{
					text2 = "Legal opposition";
					text = "The legal opposition that since the foundation of the PUWP has been sitting in our Parliament despite even Soviet advice to eliminate it, namely ";
					if (this.global1.is_party_enabled[1])
					{
						text += "individual deputies-activists of the Democratic Party ";
						if (this.global1.is_party_enabled[2])
						{
							text += "and the Polish Catholic-Social Union ";
						}
					}
					else if (this.global1.is_party_enabled[2])
					{
						text += "the Polish Catholic-Social Union ";
						if (this.global1.is_party_enabled[1])
						{
							text += "and individual deputies-activists of the Democratic Party ";
						}
					}
					text += "again, as in the early 80's, they are opposed to military measures and propose to carry out democratic reforms in the country, while our Motherland still has a chance for peaceful transformation.|For our future. For the sake of Poland.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 146)
				{
					text2 = "Poland, Mao, Socialism!";
					text = "Poland has changed. Jaruzelski and his supporters were removed from power, which means that we need to look for new political allies. Some party members suggest that the ex-Stalinist Kazimierz Mijal be dismissed from the disgrace. Mijal became famous in the history of Poland, as an ardent Stalinist and critic of power. He criticized for revisionism even Wladyslaw Gomulka (leader of Poland in the 70s), for which he was withdrawn from politics, and later fled to Albania, where he picked up Hojaism and Maoism. Mijal returned to Poland during the reign of Jaruzelski, but again began to expose the government to criticism from the standpoint of radical communism, for which he was again put in disgrace and even served a couple of months under arrest.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 162)
				{
					text2 = "OUR HEARTS REQUIRE CHANGES";
					text = "Comrade General Secretary!|Undoubtedly, our country is the best in the world, rich and prosperous, and your government is the most wise, honest and fair...|Except it seems that not everyone shares this view. Popular masses, driven by a thirst for change, have opposed you and your government... ";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 163)
				{
					text2 = "DAYS THAT SHOCKED US...";
					text = "Comrade General Secretary!|Undoubtedly, our country is the best in the world, rich and prosperous, and your government is the most wise, honest and fair...|And now let's take a look at the situation not through the prism of our official press. Popular masses, driven by discontent with the current regime, have opposed you and your government... I'm not asking you how we came to such a life. I am asking you - what shall we do?";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 164)
				{
					text2 = "PARTY CONSPIRACY?";
					text = "Comrade General Secretary!|As in any other country, there are people dissatisfied with the current state of affairs at the top of our state. Some declare their disloyalty openly, some prefer to hide it, for the time being...|Recently, you receive notices that some people and groups of people are starting to organize themselves into undefined groups. In particular, their meetings in the country house of some prominent party members have become more frequent. Is this a conspiracy against you?..";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 147)
				{
					text2 = "Democratic package";
					text = "Our country has been reforming since 1984, but there are no positive results. The old Kadar policy has outlived itself, so we need to deepen the reforms! The new package of laws will give our citizens long-awaited freedom! However, some key points of the Constitution regarding communism are being revised there ... But let's be honest with ourselves, it has outlived itself. We must move into a bright future, not stare into a dark past.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 148)
				{
					text2 = "The Iron Curtain";
					text = "The pressure from Moscow is weakening every day. It is time to establish contacts with the civilized world, if we want independence. And what can say about our peaceful intentions louder than the dismantling of fortifications? By the way, Moscow will very much appreciate such a gesture of reconciliation. The plan is extremely simple: we invite the Austrian Foreign Minister, arrange a meeting and symbolically cut a piece of barbed wire. Open borders are included in the treaty. The West will definitely appreciate such a generous gesture of our goodwill. ";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 149)
				{
					text2 = "Four reformers";
					text = "Prominent reformer leaders, who have become far more radical than Gros himself, demand that full-fledged collective rule must be introduced. These leaders are Miklos Nemeth, a young economist with American education, Rezho Nersh, a man responsible for the emergence of cadarism and all our openly market reforms and Imre Pohgai, Gorbachev's favorite and the most radical reformer of the whole trio. ";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 150)
				{
					text2 = "Opposition Knights of the Round Table";
					text = "Opposition in our country is given quite broad rights and a large number of party members are in favor of starting negotiations with it, because it can no longer be ignored. Gorbachev, like an invisible shadow, backs their words and literally pushes us to agree to the creation of the Round Table of negotiations. In the PPR, such negotiations have recently been held with the opposition. But in our country everything is much simpler, even within the party itself prominent figures openly reject communism. So why shouldn't we sit at the notorious negotiating table, since the path is already trodden for us? ";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 151)
				{
					text2 = "Great heroes";
					text = "Many party members want us to reconsider the attitude towards Imre Nagy, whom a fairly large number of people consider a national hero. They propose to rebury his remains in the Budapest Heroes' Square, as well as the remains of other participants in those old events, thereby admitting our mistakes and redeeming our guilt before these outstanding people.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 152)
				{
					text2 = "Dear Leader";
					text = "Today happened somthing irreparable... Our former leader, J. Kadar, had passed away ... Many party members, as a sign of respect, offer to solemnly bury him at the Kerepeshi cemetery. However, some saw this as a manifestation of Stalinism and the cult of personality, and therefore offered some other options. ";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 153)
				{
					text2 = "Dear non-party comrades";
					text = "After the funeral of Kadar, on the sly, there were proposals for the rehabilitation of various parts of the repressed at Kadar after the Hungarian uprising. Some offer to rehabilitate the Stalinists, others - the nationalists. But should we change the policy of the party so quickly? ";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 154)
				{
					text2 = "Volodya";
					text = "Today the USSR Ambassador came to us with a fat folder and said that the head of KGB had personally handed it to him. The folder says “Secret”, and the columns indicate that it is about Imre Nagy. The texts inside say: “In 1929-1944 Imre Nagy lived in the USSR, working in the Comintern and in the Institute of Agriculture of the Academy of Sciences of the USSR. January 17, 1933 was recruited as a secret informer GUGB NKVD of the USSR. During the purges of 1937-1938, as a result of which Bela Kun and a number of other Hungarian communists were repressed, according to documents in the archives of the KGB of the USSR, Imre Nagy actively collaborated with the NKVD and wrote denunciations of the Hungarian communists working in the Comintern. On the denunciations, signed by Imre Nagy personally, dozens of people were arrested, of whom 15 were shot or died in the camps. He had an agent's nickname “Volodya” and, as his superiors noted, he worked initiatively, skillfully and disinterestedly, did not receive any material remuneration ”.";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 155)
				{
					text2 = "Budapest School";
					text = "This philosophical trend was formed in our country in the 60s. Although its followers declare their adherence to Marxism, however, the ideas expressed by them were so far from Marx that even Janosh Kadar repressed them at one time. Now they live in the West, and many have completely rejected Marxism and even accepted liberalism. Due to reshuffle in our state, we can enlist their support, because qualified and loyal personnel are always needed and useful. ";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 156)
				{
					text2 = "Intervision";
					text = "In order to raise the prestige of our country, the party members came up with the idea to revive Intervision, which was closed due to the fact that in the 1980 the NDP could not hold it anymore. Of course, there immediately appeared those, who opposed this propousal, because it would cost us lots and lots of money. There was a heated debate about the form in which to revive the festival, who to invite, and is it worth it? Last Intervision most viewers were wathching for the sake of exclusively Western artists. Maybe we should refine the rules from ourselves and give a lot more creative freedom, give way to the kvartirniki along with famous ensembles? Maybe we should execute a little more strict performance regulations? We do not want to make the audience get bored, as back then during that 45-minute performance of the Czechoslovak actress? And some daredevil suggested switching to European standards and join Eurovision! But honestly, in terms of costs, it will be like creating your own competition... ";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 157)
				{
					text2 = "Debt pit";
					text = "Comrade! As far as you remember the IMF (International Monetary Fund) in 1982 gave us solid loans at a low interest rate, and we gladly took advantage of this opportunity. But in the second half of the 80s, interest rates on loan jumped sharply, which negatively affects the development of our economy. We recently held talks with the IMF representatives, where we were unequivocally hinted that in the case of liberalization of our economy and further reduction of ties with the socialist camp, interest rate will be lowered for us, but in case of refusal we will regret our decision. Obvious blackmail, but the times are hard and maybe we should agree with them, or will we try to get out of it by ourselves? Some party members say that they will continue to suffocate us with loans and it’s worth looking for ways to pay off the debt as soon as possible, maybe it’s worth following Romanian way? But this will undoubtedly cause mass discontent. In any case, we can no longer ignore this problem. ";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 158)
				{
					text2 = "Yhe Wind of Change";
					text = "After we got rid of the main part of the radical reformist wing, we can no longer be afraid of any unexpected reforms. But our people think completely different. The Nemet Miklos and his supporters held a solid political weight both among the party and the people, and it left a trace: neither the people nor the party want to see Gross at the leader of the country any more, especially after his defender Kadar died. And there aims two very prominent personalities. There is the favorite of a few conservatives, a representative of the interests of the military and command \"workers by the police \" Borbei Shandor, who promises to bring the country to \"real socialism \", whatever that means. And the second candidate is Tamas Kraus, a supporter of the gradual reformation of Hungarian socialism, an opponent of a market economy and a doctor of historical sciences, with his ability to find a compromise between all forces. But Gross still has some supporters and the accumulated ties in the structures, it may be advantageous to leave him the leader of the country as the “guarantor of Hungarian stability”»?";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 159)
				{
					text2 = "Hungarian lands";
					text = "Nationalism, observed throughout the reign of Ceausescu, and particularly intensified in recent times, led to clashes between the Romanians and the Hungarian minority in Transylvania, protruding for a long time, including because of the rhetoric and actions of Ceausescu. However, with his fall, the oppressed Hungarians began to organize demonstrations and organize themselves to fight the aggressive Romanians. The Romanians decided to disperse the Hungarian speeches and prepare a bill on the free migration of Hungarians to Hungary. Until 1968, Transylvania was the Hungarian autonomy, and we are simply obliged to help our brothers escape from the bondage of an alien state! ";
				}
				else if (this.global1.number_event == 160)
				{
					text2 = "Rusyns and Transcarpathia";
					text = "The new union treaty was signed and we have the opportunity to return the Rusyns under our wing. Transcarpathian Rus has been under our control for many years, and the people of the Rusyns themselves are closely associated with us, being a de-facto mixture of Eastern Slavs and Hungarians. After the First World War we lost these lands, and they moved away first to Czechoslovakia, and then to the USSR, but we can restore historical justice! This is our duty! We will take advantage of the situation and ignite a spark of popular unrest! ";
