							case 194:
								text2 = "VI army corps";
								text = "Comrade Great Leader! Urgent news from one of the officers of the VI army corps stationed in the province of North Hamgyong. Through an encrypted telegram, he gave information about the upcoming officer conspiracy against comrade Kim Jong-Il, and that the whole division is about to unite with all the Northern army units and go to Pyongyang. What will happen next?";
								break;
							case 195:
								text2 = "XIII YOUTH FESTIVAL";
								text = "Just the other day in Pyongyang there will be an event for which we have been preparing for the last 3 years the XIII world festival of youth and students. Under the wise and pragmatic leadership of your son, comrade Kim Jong-Il, more than 260 large facilities were successfully built, including the stadium \"First of May\" with a capacity of 150 thousand seats. Our southern neighbour categorically forbade sending a delegation from the Republic of Korea, fearing the growth of communist ideas and possible provocations, despite our desire to attract them. However, in the experience of other socialist countries hosting this event, we know that thousands of foreigners cannot but leave consequences in the minds of our citizens, and perhaps we should hedge by limiting contacts with foreigners for their own safety? In addition, unnecessary talk about our internal problems can undermine our international image. Or do we keep the \"spirit of the festival\" and do not do anything extra?";
								break;
							case 196:
								text2 = "Return of the legend";
								text = "Comrade " + this.global1.politics_name[this.global1.data[11]] + " perhaps you are not up to this, but our citizens, in connection with the growing economic problems, are now in despair and are no longer so revolutionary to our foreign policy enemies, as it was before. Therefore, we need to encourage the masses. For example, we can send our cartoon studios an order for the release of new propaganda series of beloved by citizens animated series \"Squirrel and Hedgehog\", in which the humanized inhabitants of the \"flower hill\" are fighting against imperialist animals mice and wolves, representing the United States and South Korea, respectively. However, due to the past youth festival, our budget is empty, and even the cost of such a project may simply not be justified, maybe it is better to import western cartoons?";
								break;
							case 197:
								text2 = "DekimIlsunganization!?";
								text = "Comrade " + this.global1.politics_name[this.global1.data[11]] + ", our Great Leader is no longer with us for several months and I think it's time to finally objectively assess his contribution to history. During the long reign of the great leader Kim Il-Sung, under his wise leadership, our country has turned from a backward feudal colony of Japan into a highly developed country, able to compete on an equal footing with the American-occupied South Korea. However, his time has passed, and your era has come the era of prosperity and well-being of our country, perhaps, it is necessary to write your name in our history? Or do we review our past and Juche ideas in particular?";
								break;
							case 198:
								text2 = "The end of the feud?";
								text = "The world is changing, and as much as we would like not to make concessions to the South Korean occupation regime, we will have to do so. The Korean war ended almost 40 years ago, but our southern neighbour and we are still in a state of confrontation, de jure. Now that the cold war is over, it is time to think about peace on the Korean Peninsula. We must finally join the UN and, through its mediation, hold two-side talks between the two Koreas. But some party members will consider such a step a betrayal and a retreat to the impending imperialist aggression and offer not to bow to the western threat. Or, maybe, peace is more important to our people?";
								break;
							case 199:
								text2 = "Extinct Sun of the Nation";
								text = "The unthinkable and the impossible has happened! Tonight from heart attack has died the Great leader comrade Kim Il Sung. The whole country mourns the death of the genius of our time and the leader of the nation. But political intrigues do not cease. Because of the sudden death of the President of the DPRK in the highest echelons of power, two influential groups are fighting among themselves for the vacant place of the head of state. On the one hand, the actual receiver of Kim Il Sung is his son Kim Jong Il, who was promoted to the heights by his father in the last years of his life. And on the other O Jin U, which with the help of generals wants to take power into their own hands and \"whip to stabilize the precarious situation in the country\". What will happen next?";
								break;
							case 200:
								text2 = "Korean question";
								text = "Over the past three years, the situation in our country has changed dramatically, but the cold war is over, and I think it is time to put an end to the separation of our people. South Korea is ready to negotiate the demilitarization of the Korean Peninsula, but perhaps we can do more and, with mutual concessions, begin a gradual reunification into a single Korean state. However, such a process will take a long time, but this vile and shameful border separating a single people will finally fall. But if Korea becomes United... who will get the power in the new state? Maybe it's better to keep your current privileged position than risk it?";
								break;
							case 201:
								text2 = "The crisis of South Korean democracy";
								text = "Over the past week, large-scale protests against corruption, the resignation of the current government and the holding of new presidential elections have erupted in neighboring South Korea. The frequent cases of self-immolation of protesters, the number of which exceeded one dozen, also add fuel to the fire. However, civil discontent gradually subsides and very soon any protests will come to naught. But with an overly anti-communist Roh Tae-woo our way, and if we secretly support the opposition Democratic party led by a more peaceful Kim Dae-jung, we can rekindle protests with renewed vigor and achieve early presidential elections. Although, if you think about it, what will change for us?";
								break;
							case 202:
								text2 = "Kosygin's legacy";
								text = "Our economy continues to be in a very poor state, so the moderate wing of our party proposes to carry out limited reforms aimed at a slight decentralization of the national economy and the transfer of certain powers to the directors of enterprises. Something similar has already been used during the eighth five-year plan in the Soviet Union under the leadership of Prime Minister Alexei Kosygin, and in socialist Hungary became a pillar of economic development. Kosygin reform, despite significant shortcomings in the form of growth of corruption frauds and the general level of prices, could well fill the Soviet budget. But the conservatives in our party are categorically against it. They call such measures \"apostasy\" and \"the actual restoration of capitalism\", so they propose to limit themselves to the return of the tax system, which was abolished in 1974 to \"patch the budget holes\". Comrade Great Leader, the last word is yours!";
								break;
							case 203:
								text2 = "Final verdict";
								text = "Recently, our country has been diligently conducting research on the historical contribution of the former leader of the DPRK, comrade Kim Il-Sung, as well as the ideas of Juche described by him. The \"Commission for victims and the repressed\", established under your personal protection, has revealed a huge number of human rights violations in the DPRK over several decades of Kim Il-Sung's rule. Now that we are aiming to democratize domestic policy and the course of warming relations with our southern neighbor, we must officially and at the state level get rid of the cult of personality of Kim Il-Sung. However, such a radical turn in relation to the former \"Sun of the nation\" people might not like. Perhaps we should pursue a more gradual policy of \"curtailing the cult of personality\" or delay it altogether?";
								break;
							case 204:
								text2 = "Betrayal of an old friend";
								text = "Comrade " + this.global1.politics_name[this.global1.data[11]] + " the unthinkable has happened! Our loyal ally - socialist China, for which we did not go to full contact with the Soviet Union and did not enter into the CMEA, curtailed the program of assistance to our country. We did not expect such a blow in the back and betrayal, and now our economy will have a very hard time. However, we still have a chance to return the \"old\" China, which has recently survived the protests in Tiananmen, and to persuade it to resume sending humanitarian aid to our country. With the help of our special services, we can eliminate the leader of the Chinese reformers Deng Xiaoping, allowing the conservatives to retain power in China. But the chances of this are small, and, perhaps, with closer relations with South Korea, we will be able to create special economic zones for their entrepreneurs, thereby replenishing the empty budget. And finally, we can rely on our strength and leave everything as it is.";
								break;
							case 205:
								text2 = "Rehabilitation";
								text = "In early 1988, the head Of the Institute of electronic warfare \"Mirim\" O Geun Nel, together with his deputy Kim Yong Chun, was sent to \"retraining\" because of the internal conflict with the current Minister of defense O Jin U. Now that the influence of the generals on the situation in the country is greater than ever, it is worth thinking about strengthening your personal power, then to successfully pass it to your son Kim Jong Il. Perhaps the best idea would be to rehabilitate these soldiers with their support. Moreover, O Kyn Nel was brought up in an orphanage and was under the care of your first wife Kim Jong-Suk and since childhood was a close friend of Kim Jong-Il, so his devotion can not be doubted. But is it worth returning Kim Yong-Chun? While working in the South Korean labor party Committee, he gained a reputation as a firm but loyal reformer. However, their return may be severely affected by the increased influence of O Jin U.";
								break;
							case 206:
								text2 = "The enemy's sanctions";
								text = "Comrade " + this.global1.politics_name[this.global1.data[11]] + " because of our recent nuclear tests, the US and its allies have imposed an embargo on North Korea for the import of fuel, a huge list of luxury goods and military technologies. Our economic situation before the sanctions was very bad, and now I can not even imagine how we can stay afloat. Western leaders demand the complete and unconditional abolition of any nuclear tests and research, as well as the return of experts from the IAEA to the country, and only in this case, trade restrictions will be lifted. However, we can strengthen trade ties with the \"remnants of the socialist camp\", but this is unlikely to help us much.";
								break;
							case 207:
								text2 = "Opening of SEZ";
								text = "The economic situation continues to deteriorate rapidly, so it is necessary to take at least some measures to stay afloat. The few of the reformist wing of our party offers to make concessions to the imperialists and \"slightly open the door\" for foreign investors, allowing them to take advantage of our cheap labor. With such measures, we will be able to fill our empty budget. In addition, we can go a little further and stop being so reckless about small business, artisans and artels, thereby expanding the economic freedoms of our population, but what will the party say? Finally, we can use the most desperate measures, such as sending to planting and construction soldiers on duty.";
								break;
							case 208:
								text2 = "The saving power of the atom";
								text = "Comrade " + this.global1.politics_name[this.global1.data[11]] + ", the Soviet Union and the socialist camp have recently been a significant part of world politics, but over the past five years the situation has changed dramatically. The Soviet Union is \"sick\" of separatism in the republics and the protracted economic crisis, and in Eastern Europe workers ' parties can not break the backbone of the growing popularity of the opposition. Perhaps we should hedge and protect ourselves against external world problems, because in the collapse of the world socialist system, we can remain unarmed. I think we need to re-establish our nuclear programme and build a test site, but what will the world community say? In addition, we have other problems in our country; maybe we can allocate this money for more socially useful needs?";
