								break;
							case 210:
								text2 = "Japanese Peace";
								text = "Our relations with Japan have always been very strained and have been mostly hostile, ranging from the Korean occupation to Japan's rapidly developing cooperation with the United States and South Korea. Now, the old days are over, the Cold War is coming to an end, and perhaps we should establish relations with our sworn enemy. Moreover, if this happens, we will gain profitable trade contacts with technologically advanced Japan, which will be beneficial for us in the face of some negative developments in our economy. However, some party members suggest taking an additional loan from neighboring China without contacting the imperialists.";
								break;
							case 211:
								text2 = "«Cuba - yes, Castro - no!»";
								text = "Comandante, street riots have been raging in Havana for several weeks against the backdrop of the impending economic crisis. The protesters demand the immediate liberalization of the domestic regime and the opening of borders. And every day the requests of the protesters are becoming more radical. Something must be done. Will we open the borders to all the discontented, as in the days of the \"Mariel crossing\", or will we use force and abandon the people's squads against the protesters? Or do we enter into negotiations with the opposition leaders and satisfy the demands of the Cuban people? Comrade Fidel, we are waiting for your decisive action! If You do not want to resort to such radical measures, then use your popularity and calm the rebellious minds of the masses.";
								break;
							case 212:
								text2 = "Banana republic";
								text = "Comrade Ochoa, the moment has come when we must decide the fate of the Cuban revolution. The planned economy of the Soviet type served us well for the development of social programs, but now even in the most Orthodox socialist countries market elements in the form of cooperatives and artels are being introduced. Perhaps, on the background of general changes, we should also join the trends of change? Moreover, the energy crisis is seriously \"choking\" our economy, and perhaps such small measures will not save it: therefore, the best idea could be a serious decentralization of the economy and the transfer of power to enterprises. In an extreme case, you can introduce a shock therapy. What do you think?";
								break;
							case 213:
								text2 = "Gorbachev's visit";
								text = "A delegation of the Soviet Union headed by General Secretary of the CPSU Mikhail Gorbachev arrived in Havana today, and an official meeting between our countries is about to take place. As you know, we have a very negative attitude to the policy of \"perestroika\" in the USSR and consider it \"apostasy to the West\" and \"restoration of capitalism\". On the background of the deteriorating situation in the socialist camp, we had a chance to criticize Gorbachev and his useless foreign policy again. However, the Soviets will clearly touch on the topic of reducing aid to Cuba, on which we depend very much. Perhaps, if we are not so radical, we will be able to reduce the losses from the Soviet decision. The last word is yours, Comandante!";
								break;
							case 214:
								text2 = "Democracy Soviet-style";
								text = "In the Soviet Union for half a dozen years \"perestroika\" thunders and democratic political transformation is happening. But now the CPSU is rapidly losing credibility, and the democratic coalition and its leader Boris Yeltsin are becoming more and more influential. In addition, thanks to the policy of glasnost, some newspaper publications led by liberals and supporters of Yeltsin, such as \"Moscow news\" and \"New time\", began an active anti-Soviet agitation, actively promoting \"Pro-Yeltsin\" ideas. The problem is that these newspapers are also published in Cuba, and our citizens read these \"harmful publications\". I propose to limit and ban their production in Cuba, so that Cubans read only domestic newspapers and do not think about the superfluous. However, by means of stricter censorship we will reduce our authority of the \"Island of freedom\". We can also counterattack and start publishing anti-perestroika newspapers, but it will have to fork out a little";
								break;
							case 215:
								text2 = "Children of Chernobyl";
								text = "Despite the reduction of aid from the Soviet Union and not the most pragmatic foreign policy of Comrade " + this.global1.sovietPremiereName + ", we are in great debt to the Soviet people, who valiantly helped us get rid of the miserable existence of the Batista era and become one of the most developed countries in Latin America. A few years ago, our Big brother had a disaster – the Chernobyl disaster. It has dealt a serious blow to the health of citizens of the region, especially children. And now the USSR appeals to all governments of the world to provide qualified assistance. It is known that our medicine is one of the leading in the world, and it is time, despite our economic difficulties, to return the favor to the Soviet people and take in seriously ill children to our sanatoriums.";
								break;
							case 216:
								text2 = "Sworn friends";
								text = "For more than thirty years since the victory of the Cuban revolution, the Island of Freedom has been living under many American sanctions and embargoes, and is even on the list of so-called \"state sponsors of terrorism\". But times are changing, the cold war was replaced by a general discharge and thaw in relations between former enemies. Maybe it is time for us to forget all old grievances and resume relations with the United States for the sake of world peace? But is it possible to forget these grievances? The atrocities that were done by the rich Americans to ordinary Cuban citizens? Perhaps it is better to stay out of this \"American world\"?";
								break;
							case 217:
								text2 = "Cuban Democracy Act";
								text = "For three decades, our country has been living under the us embargo, which the US has imposed on false charges. Now the States, under the patronage of Secretary of State James Baker and US Congressman Robert Torricelli because of our alleged aggression once again tighten sanctions against Cuba. \"Cuban Democracy Act\" is the title of an inhuman law of the United States, which was condemned even in the United Nations. It introduced multiple restrictions on trade to Cuba because of imperialists' fictional human rights violations on the \"Island of Freedom\". Our relations with the United States have always been very strained and hostile, but now, in connection with the crisis in the socialist camp, we have become more defenseless. Perhaps we should make concessions to the West, so as not to completely destroy our economy? On the other hand, we have repeatedly come into confrontation with the United States; why should we bend over them this time? Let us condemn their cruelty to the Cuban people! Or... just keep quiet?";
								break;
							case 218:
								text2 = "The End Of Comandante?!";
								text = "Comandante, we're in trouble! After our attempts to pacify the rebellious minds of our citizens, the protests have only intensified, and the demands of the rebels are becoming more radical. There are even calls for your immediate resignation and the immediate democratization of the regime. We must immediately take some action, otherwise nothing good will come out of this. What will happen next?";
								break;
							case 219:
								text2 = "Island of Freedom";
								text = "Cuba from now on is \"Island of Freedom\" again! Thanks to the support of the Cuban people, You, Comrade Ochoa, have succeeded in taking the reins of power into your own hands. But now people are waiting for changes. After all, freedom is a fundamental element of socialism? Moscow demands that we hold free and alternative parliamentary elections, as well as respect for fundamental human rights and freedoms. We will have to start liberalizing sooner or later, but if we \"Unscrew the screws\" too quickly, the people may rush into the arms of the populist opposition. Therefore, maybe restrain the reforms? Or even wait a little longer?";
								break;
							case 220:
								text2 = "Peaceful atom";
								text = "Back in the seventies, Cuba and the Soviet Union signed agreements on \"cooperation in the peaceful use of nuclear energy\", which resulted in the construction of a nuclear power plant in Cuba. It was supposed to be placed in the southern part of the province of Cienfuegos near the town of Juragua. The construction of the NPP really began in the eighties with the support of Soviet, Bulgarian and German specialists. However, plans to complete the construction were thwarted due to the curtailment of the Soviet program of assistance to the countries of the socialist camp. Now that we have a serious regime of energy saving, the power of the atom would benefit. The first reactor is almost ready, and the second half of the work has been done. If we invest in the completion of the project, the consequences of the energy crisis can be much milder for us. However, this event is not cheap: perhaps it would be better to also request the support of experts from the fraternal republics within the project? In addition, we can always just curtail the development of the project.";
								break;
							case 221:
								text2 = "Special period";
								text = "In connection with the reduction of aid from the USSR and the crisis of the socialist camp as a whole, the Cuban economy has seen the beginning of a protracted economic crisis. This is mainly due to the cessation of oil supplies from the USSR at preferential prices and the growth of electricity consumption in recent years. And the instability in fraternal countries seriously affects our economy, which is entirely focused on cooperation within the framework of the CMEA. Now our country is in hard times, and circumstances force us to introduce an analogue of the \"Romanian economy regime\" and reduce oil consumption to the minimum possible. But maybe if we manage to establish closer contacts with the countries of the socialist camp, the impact on our economy will be less painful? What do you think, Comrade " + this.global1.politics_name[this.global1.data[11]] + "?";
								break;
							case 222:
								text2 = "Big brother's betrayal";
								text = "Comandante! Our agents report that there is a brewing conspiracy against you! At the moment, it is known that there are traitors in the government who, with the assistance of the KGB, want to overthrow you and set a course for restructuring according to the Soviet type. Among them are prominent figures of the Communist party of Cuba, such as interior Ministry Colonel Tony de la Guardia and divisional General Arnaldo Ochoa. And it all happened only a month after the visit of Mikhail Sergeyevich! What will happen to our country and party in a year? It is urgent to take any measures against the conspirators! Charge them with corruption and shoot them? Or put them under house arrest to avoid unnecessary dissemination of information?";
								break;
							case 223:
								text2 = "Rome does not pay to traitors";
								text = "Rafael del Pino is a well-known Cuban politician and participant of the Cuban revolution, who under the influence of the perestroika in the USSR began active criticism against the socialist government in Cuba, and then fled to the United States, from where he now continues to broadcast about the dictatorship in Cuba and the cruel actions of the government. And it seems that he is really \"crazy\", as he accuses us of all the troubles of mankind – from drug trafficking to the sale of people into slavery. The worst thing is that Western people believe all this flow of lies. All our \"foreign opposition\" admires del Pino, undermining the prestige of Cuba and the Cuban revolution. Maybe it is time to finally shut him up. We must instruct our agents to eliminate this traitor for the future of Cuba! But what will the West and the USSR say? The murder charges are going to fall on us. As another way out, we can show the world our democracy and disband del Pino to return home. Your opinion, comrade?";
								break;
							case 224:
								text2 = "Paradise island";
								text = "From the Declaration of Independence from Spain in 1898 to the great Cuban revolution, big share of our budget came from the tourism industry - despite the plight of our people, the rampant banditry and the drug trade in Batista's time! Of course, we do not want to go back to the time when our country was a \"brothel for Americans\", but hard times require hard decisions. Perhaps, if we open the doors for Western tourists, we will be able to replenish our empty budget. But don't people pick up from the visitors any bourgeois remnants? Perhaps it would be better to limit the access of our citizens to such events? Or even abandon this idea?";
								break;
							case 225:
								text2 = "Socialism or barbarism?";
								text = "Comrade " + this.global1.politics_name[this.global1.data[11]] + " as you know, the next Congress of the Communist party of Cuba will be held soon. At the previous III Congress, the main goal of the party was to complete the construction of socialism on the basis of Marxist-Leninist ideology. However, times are changing, and on the background of general changes in Eastern Europe, we may need to reconsider our views on socialism and the dictatorship of the proletariat. To remain faithful to the ideals of Leninism or to abandon the secondary positions of Marxism? Or maybe go to Eurocommunism or even, abandoning all the dogmas, take the position of social democracy? The last word is yours.";
								break;
							case 226:
								text2 = "SEZ";
								text = "The economic situation continues to deteriorate rapidly, so it is necessary to take at least some measures to stay afloat. The few of the reformist wing of our party offers to make concessions to the imperialists and \"slightly open the door\" for foreign investors, allowing them to take advantage of our cheap labor. With such measures, we will be able to fill our empty budget. In addition, we can go a little further and stop being so reckless about small business, artisans and artels, thereby expanding the economic freedoms of our population, but what will the party say?";
								break;
							case 227:
								break;
							case 228:
								text2 = "II Congress of the PDPA";
								text = "As you know, the other day, the II Congress of the People’s Democratic Party of Afghanistan should be held. In the eyes of a significant part of the masses, our party has a very bad reputation, some mujahideen leaders call it completely pro-Kremlin, and the presidential regime is imposed from without. If we completely get rid of the dark past of our party and move on to a full-fledged multi-party system, then perhaps we can come to a compromise with a moderate part of the opposition. However, this event is unlikely to please the Khalq wing of our party, which require only moderate Islamic reforms within the framework of socialism. What are we going to do, president?";
								break;
							case 229:
								text2 = "End of war";
								text = "Thanks to the pragmatic work of the government and the enthusiasm of the working people of Afghanistan, the situation in Afghanistan is gradually stabilizing, and peaceful life is returning to cities and villages. The civil war is behind, however, local centers of the opposition continue to partisan in remote mountainous areas, sometimes making sorties and brutally cracking down on civilians. Now that the mujahideen are most vulnerable and deprived of arms supplies from Pakistan, it is time to deliver a decisive blow to the opposition and put an end to this dark period of our history. However, we can, under certain conditions, agree with the mujahideen and offer them freedom, in exchange for their leaders. Or will we use the fragmentation of the opposition and drag more moderate and more loyal people to their leadership? It’s also worth trying to make contact with the unofficial partisan leader Ahmad Shah Massoud, but would he agree? In any case, we can just wait for the mujahideen leaders to fight each other. What do you think about it, president?";
								break;
							case 230:
								text2 = "Negotiations";
								text = "Today, official negotiations between the government of Afghanistan and the moderate part of the opposition, in the person of the Pashtun spiritual leader Sibghatullah Mojaddedi and the chairman of the \"Jamiat-e Islami\" Burhanuddin Rabbani, should take place. The opposition requires us to hold free parliamentary elections, as well as the organization of a coalition government with «Watan» and other Islamic parties that refuse to cooperate with us. The remaining Khalqists, however, demand that we stop negotiations and shoot down the enemies of the people. What are we going to do, president?";
								break;
							case 231:
								text2 = "«Typhoon»";
								text = "Comrade Najibullah, as you know, in a month, the last division of the Soviet troops will be withdrawn from the territory of our country. The Soviet leadership made the decision to conduct the last large-scale operation, the purpose of which is to inflict maximum damage on the positions of Ahmad Shah Massoud in the north-eastern zone of Afghanistan before the start of the withdrawal of Soviet troops. However, the use of massive aerial bombardments can cause serious damage not only to militants, but also to civilians, which in turn will put us in a not very advantageous position before the mujahideen. Moreover, according to the conclusion of the Geneva agreements, we have an agreement with Ahmad Shah, according to which he will not interfere with the withdrawal of troops, and we will not fight with him. Perhaps we should try to persuade the Soviet leadership not to carry out the operation, what do you think, president?";
								break;
							case 232:
								text2 = "Homeland of terrorism";
								text = "Pakistan agreed to hold regular negotiations with the government of Afghanistan to cut off the supply of weapons to the mujahideen, and today a meeting of delegations should be held in Tashkent, under the guardianship of the USSR and the USA. Our goal in these negotiations, by hook or by crook, is to force the Pakistani side to comply with the Geneva agreements, as well as to force Islamabad to allow Afghan refugees to return to their homeland and not to hinder this. However, the United States, which violates international law by supporting the mujahideen, is unlikely to agree to our conditions, and in any case we will have to make concessions. I hope gracious Allah will help us in a good deed for all Afghan peoples.";
								break;
							case 233:
								text2 = "Land to the peasants, factories to the workers, sanctions to Pakistan!";
								text = "Pakistan, since the beginning of the civil war in our country, has been actively supporting (not without the help of the United States) the Afghan mujahideen. Disregarding the results of last year's Geneva Accords, under which Afghanistan and Pakistan settled the principles of relations between the countries, especially the principle of non-interference in each other's internal affairs, Pakistan continues to send reinforcements to help the opposition. In June 1988, for example, Pakistani President Zia-ul-Haq declared that the Geneva Accords were “nothing but a fig leaf.” Now that Pakistan has suffered a string of defeats at the hands of India, especially after the recent treacherous attack on its eastern neighbor, we could enlist the support of our CMEA allies and impose economic sanctions on Pakistan for non-compliance with the accords, which in turn would irreparably damage the weak Pakistani economy. However, the socialist camp is in the midst of changes, and will they pay attention to us? In any case, we can once again appeal to the USSR to pressurize Pakistan, but will it bring any results? Or shall we continue the \"viscous struggle\" with the insurgents?";
								break;
							case 234:
								text2 = "Exiled king";
								text = "Mohammed Zahir Shah, the former king of Afghanistan, who ruled the country for 40 years and was ousted after the military coup of his cousin Mohammed Daoud, is still popular with the nationalist wing of the mujahideen, despite the fact that he has been in immigration for more than 15 years. Some members of the government propose lifting the ban on the return of the deposed monarch to Afghanistan, which was introduced under Taraki, thereby showing the right opposition the readiness to cooperate. Moreover, if we legalize the National Liberation Front of Afghanistan, the stronghold of all moderate royalists and monarchists, we will deal a serious blow to the ranks of the opposition. However, party members are categorically against such strong concessions and demand to abandon this initiative. The last word is yours.";
								break;
							case 235:
								text2 = "Khalq";
								text = "As you know, during the great Saur Revolution, the main part in the coup was taken by the radical wing of the People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan - the Khalq, under the leadership of Taraki and Amin. After the coming of power, the parchamist Babrak Karmal began mass persecution against the leaders and members of the Khalq and now, after the withdrawal of Soviet troops, the confrontation in the party reached a boiling point. According to the Ministry of State Security, some of the Khalq's generals whose secret leader is Defense Minister Shahnawaz Tanai are plotting against you. It is necessary to take measures - to arrest the traitors! But in this case, Tanai can oppose us on behalf of all the khalqists, maybe we need to throw in information about his cooperation with the mujahideen, which our intelligence agencies suggest, thereby getting rid of the unstable wing in the PDPA? Anyway, we can just wait.";
								break;
							case 236:
								text2 = "Tanai's rebellion";
								text = "There is a conspiracy in Afghanistan! Defense Minister Tanai, in agreement with the generals, organized an armed rebellion against you! 20 tanks were deployed to Kabul from Pul-e-Charkhi, and the 8th infantry division was sent from the north-west, and they were about to enter Kabul. What will happen next?";
								break;
							case 237:
								text2 = "The Last Day of Pompeii";
								text = "President, our situation continues to worsen! The opposition breaks through our last frontiers, the soldiers of the people's army surrender and go over to the side of the mujahideen and the attack on Kabul is unfolding in full swing. We need to take some measures, otherwise our Motherland will end! We mobilize the capital, giving the citizens the remnants of weapons, but it is unlikely that untrained workers will be able to oppose anything against professional mujahideen. Or... Again we call the socialist camp, requesting military support? In any case, we can only rely on the will of Allah.";
								break;
							case 238:
								text2 = "The best defense is an attack";
								text = "President " + this.global1.politics_name[this.global1.data[11]] + ", the opposition is on the alert and ready to strike at any moment, maybe we should get ahead of it? If you think that you are ready enough for a counterattack, then we can organize it, but keep in mind that the rebels are too strong and we will have to send reinforcements to help our army for some more time. Where are we advancing, comrade?";
								break;
							case 239:
								text2 = "Many a little makes a mickle";
								text = "After the withdrawal of Soviet troops, our situation continues to worsen, and although the USSR continues to supply us with military and humanitarian assistance, the strength and influence of the opposition continues to grow steadily. Since rebels and counter-revolutionaries are supported by the United States and Pakistan, why not ask for help from our allies? Yes, now crisis phenomena are observed in the Warsaw Pact countries, but if we manage to persuade our partners to supply assistance, then the position of the government army will seriously strengthen. In any case, we can once request assistance from the USSR. What do you think, president?";
								break;
							case 240:
								text2 = "Troop withdrawal";
								text = "According to the 1988 Geneva Agreements, the Soviet Union, for 9 months, was required to completely withdraw a limited contingent of Soviet troops from Afghanistan. And today, the last division of the Soviet army under the leadership of Lieutenant General Gromov must cross the Soviet-Afghan border. For the whole 9 years the USSR has provided us with all sorts of humanitarian and military support, it is necessary to thank the fraternal people for the assistance provided to us, isn't it?";
								break;
							case 241:
								if (this.global1.data[90] != 0 || this.global1.data[92] != 0 || this.global1.data[93] != 0 || this.global1.data[94] != 0)
								{
									int num = -1;
									while (this.global1.data[num + 90] != 1)
									{
										this.global1.data[107] = global::UnityEngine.Random.Range(1, 5);
										if (this.global1.data[107] > 1)
										{
											num = this.global1.data[107];
										}
										else
										{
											num = 0;
										}
									}
								}
								else if (this.global1.data[81] == 1)
								{
									this.global1.data[107] = 1;
								}
								text2 = "Rebellious mountainous region";
								text = "President " + this.global1.politics_name[this.global1.data[11]] + ", the opposition does not give rest to our people! This time, the opposition invaded the ";
								if (this.global1.data[107] == 1)
								{
									text += "East ";
								}
								else if (this.global1.data[107] == 2)
								{
									text += "West ";
								}
								else if (this.global1.data[107] == 3)
								{
									text += "North ";
								}
								else if (this.global1.data[107] == 4)
								{
									text += "South ";
								}
								text += "region of Afghanistan and begins to sow anarchy there, killing civilians and officials. We must immediately take action and free our motherland from the damned mob. However, we will not destroy the opposition by one strike, and we will have to send reinforcements more than once to help the Afghan army, but in any case, glory to the Democratic Republic of Afghanistan!";
								break;
							case 242:
								text2 = "Eternal revoltuion";
								text = "Comandante! Viva Cuba! Today, exactly 30 years ago, the dictatorial regime of Fulgencio Batista was overthrown during the Cuban Revolution, and popular democracy finally triumphed on our island. In honor of this grand event, millions of Cubans are waiting for your inspirational speech, which will mark the logical conclusion of the celebration of the thirtieth anniversary of the popular uprising. However, the international political situation has changed dramatically: perestroika is underway in the Soviet Union, and the Cold War is coming to an end, as a result of which too radical and careless sayings can cause a flurry of criticism from either the United States or the USSR. In this regard, Gorbachev might not like the assessment of the events of 1959 and the future world revolution, and before his upcoming visit to Cuba, one does not want to spoil relations with the Union. Could it be better to shift the vector of appeal to the people in favor of the need for social change? It will be to the liking of the USSR.";
								break;
							case 243:
								text2 = "Spirit of an era";
								text = "Comrade " + this.global1.politics_name[this.global1.data[11]] + "! It is has been three years, since we, with some success, have started the construction of the 105-story Ryugong Hotel, which was originally planned to be our answer to the construction of the Stamford Hotel in Singapore by a South Korean company, and was supposed to be included in the program of the XIII Festival of Youth and Students. However, due to financial problems, the construction of the hotel was being constantly delayed. Right now, our situation isn't the best, and some Party members are suggesting to halt construction, freezing the project's completion. But this high rise has cost us so many efforts, so, maybe the best idea in this situation would be - to hand over construction to private organizations? What do you think?";
								break;
							case 244:
								text2 = "Agrarian question";
								text = "Due to the fact that over the past 30 years, our agriculture has been seriously interconnected with trade within the CMEA, the destruction of traditional economic contacts has caused significant damage to this industry. In this regard, experts from the State Planning Commission and our economists suggest tackling a radical solution to this issue. Some party members suggest adopting the experience of China, transferring land into the ownership of family contracts, thereby legalizing private trade. Opponents of this idea suggest strengthening the influence of collective farm associations by giving them additional loans for mechanization, but will this help, given the serious energy crisis? The most liberal comrades-in-arms suggest completely discarding all the ideals of Marxism-Leninism, transferring the land to private property or even to foreign investors.";
								break;
							case 245:
								text2 = "The collapse of anti-imperialism?";
								text = "Comrade, as you know, we have been in contact with the Mengistu Haile Mariam regime for a long time, helping the Ethiopian leader repel the attacks of the opposition and Eritrean nationalists. In this regard, our divisions have long been on the territory of Ethiopia, but unlike Angola, in which the internal situation is also unstable, the ruling party of the RPE continues to lose control of the country. Due to the fact that a serious crisis is beginning in our country, some advisers demand from us decisiveness: should we withdraw troops from Ethiopia or support the unpopular regime of Haile Mariam? Some of our military men, who do not believe in the further fate of Ethiopia under the rule of the Workers' Party, offer to support the opposition at all led by the Revolutionary Democratic Party. What do you think, comrade?";
								break;
							case 246:
								text2 = "The party split";
								text = "Comrade " + this.global1.politics_name[this.global1.data[11]] + ", after the withdrawal of Soviet troops from the territory of our country, the inter-factional split between radical Khalqists and more moderate Parchamists became more acute in our party. As a result of this, the party’s course is constantly changing due to extremely different views and opinions on the situation in Afghanistan. The preponderance of forces in the party is constantly changing, so some members of the opposing faction are preparing a conspiracy against you. We must act decisively, otherwise it will be too late! We had a great chance to smash and arrest our ideological opponents, but will this not be a catalyst for an even greater catastrophe? Can we manage to solve the problem by more democratic methods?";
								break;
							case 247:
								text2 = "Is there such a party?";
								text = "Despite all our attempts to stabilize the situation in the party and at the front, the situation continues to be extremely deplorable: the opposition does not even think to cooperate, and the bickering continues between the Khalqists and the Parchamists in the party. In this regard, confidence in our party has been undermined, and many adherents of the PDPA are switching to the side of the mujahideen. For a long time, the military, as well as some statesmen, have been demanding the creation of a non-party government, independent of the PDPA, but loyal to the Soviet leadership. Most party members and members of the government advocate the appointment of the influential military Abdul Rashid Dostum to the post of president of the country, while the administration of the party apparatus will remain entirely with " + this.global1.politics_name[this.global1.data[11]] + ". However, is it worth trusting him and what will happen next?";
								break;
							case 248:
								text2 = "Land reform";
								text = "After the April Revolution, the Khalq leadership decided to start a full-scale agrarian reform, the purpose of which was to redistribute the lands of large feudal lords for the free use of peasant families. However, despite good intentions, the reform failed. In the eyes of the conservative population, such a policy was an encroachment of the government on private property, sanctified in their minds by religion and centuries-old traditions, and as a result, armed anti-government opposition gained strength in Afghanistan. The reform was condemned and canceled back in 1986, but no significant changes followed, maybe it is time to return to the agrarian question again? Our party is dominated by several opinions about this: an unshakable continuation of support for the small land peasantry, a review of the results of the reform of 1978, and the beginning of a course to promote cooperation on agricultural land. However, do not forget that a sharp change in the party line can lead to corresponding unrest in society.";
								break;
							case 249:
								text2 = "Course change";
								text = "The situation in the country remains extremely difficult, and the influence of the mujahideen is constantly growing. The opposition has refused to negotiate with the government, desertion is spreading on the front, and the decline in Soviet aid has hit our economy hard. The situation has therefore forced us to seek help from other countries. Perhaps the main alternative is India, which was one of the first countries to recognize the new revolutionary government of Afghanistan and maintained full contacts with us throughout the 1980s, seeing us as one of the main allies to counter Pakistan. Another alternative is China, which used to help the opposition, but in recent years this assistance has decreased. A third option is the Arab countries, but it is not clear whether we have any real allies in the region who are able to help. In any case, in such a desperate situation, any support provided could help.";
								break;
							case 250:
								text2 = "Popular unrest";
								if (this.global1.data[90] != 0 || this.global1.data[92] != 0 || this.global1.data[93] != 0 || this.global1.data[94] != 0)
								{
									int num2 = -1;
									while (this.global1.data[num2 + 90] != 1)
									{
										this.global1.data[56] = global::UnityEngine.Random.Range(1, 5);
										if (this.global1.data[56] > 1)
										{
											num2 = this.global1.data[56];
										}
										else
										{
											num2 = 0;
										}
									}
								}
								else if (this.global1.data[81] == 1)
								{
									this.global1.data[56] = 1;
								}
								text = "Comrade President! ";
								if (this.global1.data[56] == 1)
								{
									text += "East ";
								}
								else if (this.global1.data[56] == 2)
								{
									text += "West ";
								}
								else if (this.global1.data[56] == 3)
								{
									text += "North ";
								}
								else if (this.global1.data[56] == 4)
								{
									text += "South ";
								}
								text += " region flared up. In the region, due to growing dissatisfaction with the actions of the government, a large-scale uprising began. Militia and people's squads cannot pacify the armed crowd, which, moreover, begins to rob civilians. Protest leaders are demanding higher wages, resolving the issue of peace and easing censorship, but now we have a hard time, and any easing can seriously affect the situation in the country and only warm up the protests, which, in the end, the opposition can take advantage of. But here is the main question: carrot or stick?";
								break;
							case 251:
								text2 = "Conservative revolution";
								text = "Comrade " + this.global1.politics_name[this.global1.data[11]] + ", as you know, things are bad here, but in the socialist camp is even worse. The economic crisis, coupled with a reduction in material assistance from the USSR, caused serious damage to the political situation in the countries of the Eastern Bloc. The countless changes in government, the struggle of the communist parties against the liberal opposition, as well as pressure from Gorbachev, set a precedent for European integration and the fall of the Iron Curtain. But if the Warsaw bloc ceases to exist, then whose help can our country rely on? Who, if not our brothers in socialism, can counter the Western threat? We must mobilize all our forces in order to provide reciprocal support to the allied countries by establishing a Conservative coalition that can conquer the revolution. But at the same time, in the neighboring Soviet Union, there is a fierce struggle between supporters of independence and adherents of preserving the Union, and perhaps it is worth helping conservative politicians in the Supreme Council in their fight with the opposition? Or is it worth it to tackle your internal problems?";
								break;
							case 252:
								text2 = "Prisoners uprising";
								text = "Comrade " + this.global1.politics_name[this.global1.data[11]] + ", I have to tell you bad news. An uprising took place in one of the mujahideen prisoners war camps, and serious amount of weapons were taken by the rebels. After clashes with the garrisons of our army, the rebels were forced to split up, and now they pose a serious threat to nearby villages. Some villages have already received information about attacks on local residents by armed rebel gangs. It goes without saying that we should send militia units in search of rebels, but should we think about the future? The war lasts too long and the number of prisoners of war on both sides is very large, so maybe we’ll start the hostage exchange between the government and the mujahideen? However, some may perceive this as the apostasy and weakness of our army and power, and the number of militants on the opposite side will only increase. Perhaps we will return an uncompromising and trouble-free way to get rid of the problem - «Take no prisoners»? But in this case, we should expect a response. What do you think?";
								break;
							case 253:
								text2 = "Their struggle";
								text = "In Nicaragua, a bloody civil war has been raging for 10 years between the government of the Sandinista National Liberation Front, supported by the Soviet Union, and the opposition government of Contras, sponsored by the United States and its allies. Despite recent government successes in the offensive against the counter-revolutionaries, against the backdrop of perestroika changes in Soviet foreign policy, the Sandinista leadership was forced to negotiate a peaceful settlement. And just a month later, the presidential election will take place, in which, most likely, the SNLF and its leader Daniel Ortega, due to the prolonged economic crisis, will lose to the National Opposition Union led by Violeta Chamorro. We have been helping the government of Nicaragua from the very beginning of the war, and, I think, with the help of our intelligence networks, we could increase their chances of victory. Or will we support the opposition by showing our pro-democratic intentions to the whole world? Alternatively, we can completely ignore the elections.";
								break;
							case 254:
								text2 = "Things are heating up";
								text = "Comrade, unexpectedly for us, in the center of Pyongyang, from all over the city, protesters began to converge, demanding the democratization of the political system, and now more than 200 thousand people gathered in the main square of the country. The rebels easily dealt with the posts of militia who were patrolling the street. Then, breaking through the defenses, they were able to get to The monument to the ideas of Juche, on top of which were hung the flags of South and North Korea, and the latter was missing the red star. We saw such a reception during the protests in Romania, it seems that reactionary and counter-revolutionary forces have reached our country! Our party Propaganda department eats its rice in vain, since they were able to miss the obvious recruitment of our citizens by the forces of the West. In any case, it is necessary to take some decisive measures: disperse the demonstration and destroy any stronghold of the opposition in the form of other parties in our parliament? I think this is a good idea, you do not want to return bourgeois democracy to our «Paradise island», right?";
								break;
							case 255:
								text2 = "The mystery of the socialist atom";
								text = "Former times are a thing of the past, and hopes for the patronage of the USSR is meaningless, given the change of the vector of domestic and foreign policy by Mikhail Gorbachev. Now that our country can remain an orphan in the cruel world of imperialism, we need to look for any means that will help us defend our sovereignty. Of course, I don’t hint at anything, but for 30 years, with varying success, we have been conducting research in the field of nuclear energy, which can play into our hands. However, it is worth noting that despite the successes achieved in this area, we completely lack blueprints for nuclear weapons. Nevertheless, we can mobilize our intelligence networks and contact Socialist Romania on this issue. Or we can bribe officials in a country that is not part of the NPT - India, and thereby get the cherished achievements. In any case, we can limit ourselves to the purchase of new weapons from the socialist camp and thereby establish the production of our models according to the finished model. Or do we also need to join the global trend of «reconciliation» ?";
								break;
							case 256:
								text2 = "The collapse of CMEA";
								text = "Recently, the Council for Mutual Economic Assistance was dissolved, as a result of which the Cuban economy has lost its traditional trade allies and markets for its goods. In the current situation, when the socialist camp is on the verge of destruction, we need to move away from our principles and somehow compensate for this loss. On one hand, we can establish trade with third world countries, but will it help? But on the other hand, our economists argue that the restoration of Cuba's membership in the Organization of American States, from which we were expelled during the introduction of the American embargo, will greatly benefit our economy by integrating it into trade relations between North and South America. However, who is waiting for us there? After all, the OAS is completely controlled by the United States, is it worth it to once again be under their protection? Or will we maintain our sovereignty and reject these proposals?";
								break;
							case 257:
								text2 = "Beloved daughter";
								text = "Comrade Castro, it's time to tackle family employment issues. As you know, your daughter Mariela recently began her (unsurprisingly) rapid career growth in the Communist Party. Her position ... is very different from the usual line of the party, as her views ... are quite exotic for our country. She is a zealous supporter of the Eurocommunist approach to Marxism, third wave feminism, postmodernism and other idealistic nonsense that is gaining popularity in the West, separating the workers and denying the primacy of the class struggle over everything. In addition, Mariela sympathizes with Mikhail Gorbachev and Soviet perestroika, which is very alarming. Father's duty obliges you to help your daughter in her “successful” career by appointing her to some vacant ministerial post. However, will such changes in the party be supported by the government? Perhaps the fate of the revolution is paramount for you?";
								break;
							case 258:
								text2 = "Problems inside";
								text = "Comrade President. Problems are emerging within our Party. As you know, today the Ministry of Internal Affairs is headed by Sayed Gulabzoy, a very tough, independent, decisive, ambitious person, straightforward in actions and judgments, intolerant of other people's opinions if they are at odds with his own. After you came to power, he, already previously intolerant to the moderate faction Parcham, began with all zeal to break the existing balance of power within the Party. And if earlier he simply did not allow the parchamists to occupy any important positions in the MIA, now he is openly preparing a conspiracy against the parchamists, intending to remove them all from their command posts. But balance is a fragile thing, and according to the old agreement, we must divide posts equally between our factions. Maybe it is worth removing Gulabzoy before he does trouble? Instead, it is proposed to appoint either Raza Paktin, the Minister of Water Resources and Energy, who can help us come to an agreement with the tribes, but who is not sufficiently aware of the work of the MIA. Or Assadullah Sarwari, an old acquaintance of Watanjar from the time of serving to Taraki. Sarwari is now in disgrace, but his actions were particularly cruel. And now he will not even mind torturing our enemies in the name of the stability of our power!";
								break;
							case 259:
								text2 = "Kandahar";
								text = "Nur ul-Haq Ulumi has long been the head of the province with the city of Kandahar. Even Soviet specialists noted the effectiveness of his work - the activity of the Mujahideen here was minimal, the situation was stable, and the population received the PDPA warmly. However, his policy also has its drawbacks - Ulumi often did not obey the decisions of the Central Committee, refused to use violent methods, entered into negotiations with the Mujahideen and supplied them with food and finance in exchange for a truce. Moreover, Ulumi is a member of the Parcham faction and, earlier as a military leader, actively advocated the parchamization of the army and the withdrawal of all Khalqists from the armed forces. Now that we, the Khalqists, are in power, Ulumi is showing more and more insubordination and, now, demands to give him autonomy in Kandahar for independent internal political control of the region. But be careful, he has some relatives in the in the Ministry of Defense and General Staff.";
								break;
							default:
								switch (number_event)
								{
								case 363:
									text2 = "Telman will live!";
									text = "On June 19, 1972, the President of the State Council and the Council of Ministers of Cuba, Fidel Castro Ruz, paid a state visit to the GDR and presented the head of the latter, Erich Honecker, with a geographical map on which Cayo Ernest Thaelmann (Ernst Thaelmann Island) and Playa RDA (GDR Beach) were indicated. The renaming of this part of the Cayos Blancos del Sur island chain in the Caribbean Sea was secured by decree No. 3676/72 of the President of the Republic, Oswaldo Dorticos Torrado, and a bust of Telman was also installed on the beach of the island. Now that East Germany has been liquidated and its leaders are threatened with imprisonment (in the same cells in which they once sat under the Nazis), a group of conservative party members are offering to evacuate them to this island and establish a government-in-exile on it in exchange for introducing us some secret SED accounts and access to Stasi agents. The plan provides for the penetration of DI agents into Berlin under the guise of tourists and the abduction of all the functionaries of interest to us with the assistance of Erich Mielke and cosmonaut Sigmund Jähn, but the situation is complicated by the fact that Honecker and his wife are now in the Soviet military hospital in Belitsa (Soviet officers refuse to extradite them and do not let West German prosecutors go there), and his further fate is decided by Mikhail Gorbachev. If we are on good terms with the USSR, then we could ask the Soviet leader to let Honecker go to us for treatment. Or, in order to avoid the interest of the FRG in this object (voices have already begun to be heard that this island is part of the GDR and therefore should be annexed to the FRG), we can simply announce that the transfer of the island was a «formal ceremony» and it still remains within Cuba.";
									break;
								case 367:
									text2 = "La tua rivoluzione";
									text = "Against the background of changes that are increasingly embracing Europe, changes are taking place not only in the East, but also in the West. They do not bypass our western neighbor Italy. Thus, the Italian Socialist Party, headed by Bettino Craxi, which is now in the ruling coalition on the sidelines, plans to take over the entire left Italian political field and create a front of «Social Unity», which will be able to entice a large number of supporters of the PCI, torn by the confrontation of the «neo-Stalinist» and «right» wings of the party. Of course, they are now headed by a young reformist candidate Akille Okketo, a direct protege of the last Secretary General, but it is not clear how he will turn the party further. At the same time, active police measures are continuing against the remnants of the Red Brigades, whose leadership announced the self-dissolution of the organization last year. It is possible for us to have some influence on the degradation of the communist idea in Italy that is taking place now, but does it make sense, against the background of much bigger problems inside our own country?";
									break;
								case 368:
									text2 = "Northern League";
									text = "Against the backdrop of a left-wing decline, local separatism and autonomism are rising in Italy, most pronounced in the «Lombard League» with the charismatic Umberto Bossi, a former member of the Communist Party, which he left because the local Central Committee ignored Lombard identity issues. Despite some marginality, in general, they advocate the federalization of Italy and its greater decentralization, which is increasingly supported by ordinary citizens of predominantly northern regions, who are actually forced to pay for the development of the Italian south. It is noteworthy that they are in favor of the rights of national minorities, including the Italian Slovenes, one way or another oppressed at the moment by Rome. If we wanted, we could get in touch with the «League» and give them some support, but is the game worth the candle?";
									break;
								case 369:
									text2 = "Publication of operation «Gladio»";
									text = "Interesting news comes from Italy. Prime Minister Giulio Andreotti, in a public interview, acknowledged the existence of Operation Gladio, according to which Italian troops, along with the CIA, actively cooperated with a number of right-wing and extremist organizations against communists and left-wing politicians. According to the minister, a number of Italian military men joined in 1964 the Allied Secret Committee, created in 1957 by the United States, France, Belgium and Greece, and which was responsible for directing Gladio operations. A list of 622 civilians directly involved in the work on this operation was also published. After that, Andreotti declared that all weapons caches prepared in case of a Soviet invasion were destroyed, and at the same time said that Gladio was not involved in any of the terrorist attacks in Italy. The Soviet Union is still silent and does not comment on what happened, like most other socialist countries. But how will Yugoslavia react to this news?";
									text = "Interesting news is coming from Italy. In a public interview, Prime Minister Giulio Andreotti acknowledged the existence of Operation Gladio, according to which Italian troops, together with the CIA, actively cooperated with a number of right-wing and extremist organizations against communists and leftist politicians. According to the minister, a number of Italian military joined the Secret Allied Committee in 1964, created in 1957 by the United States, France, Belgium and Greece, and which was responsible for directing Gladio operations. A list of 622 civilians who were directly involved in the work on this operation was also published. After that, Andreotti said that all the weapons caches prepared in case of a Soviet invasion had been destroyed, and at the same time said that Gladio was not involved in any of the terrorist acts in Italy. The Soviet Union is still silent and does not comment on what happened, like most other socialist countries. But how will he react to this news";
									if (this.global1.data[0] == 49 || this.global1.data[0] == 50 || this.global1.data[0] == 51)
									{
										text += "Yugoslavia?";
									}
									else
									{
										text = text + this.global1.allcountries[this.global1.data[0]].name + "?";
									}
									break;
								case 370:
									text2 = "Disaster «Moby Prince»";
									text = "Urgent news! Today there was another catastrophe off the coast of Italy! Yesterday we heard the news that off the coast of Genoa there was the largest man-made disaster in the history of the country, when the oil tanker MT Haven exploded and, according to various estimates, flooded up to 50,000 tons of crude oil into the sea. Today, the «Moby Prince» ferry crashed near Livorno with another oil tanker «Agip Abruzzo», which has already led to the death of more than 100 people and a possible oil spill of up to 1000 tons. At the moment, the cause of the tragedy is unknown, one of the main versions was the thick fog located in the port. At the same time, some eyewitnesses report some warships and their strange behavior at the time of the collision. How should we respond to this catastrophe?";
									break;
								case 371:
									text2 = "Salus populi suprema lex esto";
									text = "Due to a series of failures that have befallen Italian society over the past few years, the cup of patience of the people dissatisfied with the policies of the CDP was overwhelmed, and people began to take to the streets, led by various left and right organizations. The trigger was published in a number of newspapers compromising information on the current Prime Minister Giulio Andreotti about his active ties with the mafia, as well as involvement in the murder of opposition journalists. A few days later, similar messages began to appear against other members of the Christian Democratic Party. The government has so far kept the situation under control, completely refuting rumors about any connection between CDP activists and criminals, but their situation is extremely difficult.";
									break;
								}
								break;
							}
						}
						else
						{
							switch (number_event)
							{
							case 380:
								text2 = "Mafiusu";
								text = "The problem of organized crime has always been quite acute in Italy. After the end of the Second World War, where many Sicilian bosses openly opposed the Nazis, for many years the government simply turned a blind eye to the mafia problem, and those who did not, could count on help in changing their minds. However, today the situation is beginning to improve. Since 1986, the so-called «Maxi Trial» has been going on in Italy, according to which hundreds of people, one way or another connected with the mafia, have already been convicted, including several high-ranking bosses, for more than 2000 years. However, despite some progress in this case, immediately after the heavy sentences were passed, a wave of appeals began, thanks to which many honest and not very citizens managed to protest the decision against them. For our part, we could help the Italian authorities in resolving this age-old problem, with various consultations and agent connections through the law enforcement agencies of our countries. At the same time, some of our intelligence officers, on the contrary, believe that the pacification of internal political life in Italy, which will occur as a result of these processes, can undermine the growing mood in Italian society and therefore we could help eliminate the most zealous judges participating in this process and demanding maximum punishment. for criminals.";
								break;
							case 381:
								text2 = "Libertas";
								text = " After the Risorgimento, San Marino has always had close ties with Italy, with which it has a continuous border and is located, as it were, inside its territory. That is why, when the ideas of socialism began to capture the minds of Italian citizens, similar processes began to take place in the proud independent republic. Due to the strong communist resistance during the Second World War, the local Communist Party managed to gain a fairly firm foothold in local political life and even for a long time to be in ruling coalitions with the Socialists. The popularity of the party was so high that it did not decrease even after the actual Italian invasion in 1957, when the local Christian party, under pressure from Rome, took power into its own hands. At the same time, being heavily dependent on the Italian Communist Party, the Eurocommunist wing became increasingly influential, as a result of which in the 70s and 80s it created a ruling coalition with the Christian Democrats. Now, after a rather serious success in the elections the year before last, where the Communist Party managed to gain 29% of the vote, it again entered into a coalition with the HDP. Against the background of an increasing departure from socialism in the countries of the Eastern camp and the perestroika processes in the Italian Communist Party, many San Marino party members are seriously considering a complete transition to social democratic positions, with a departure from Marxism and communism in general, in order to get even more votes. At the same time, the positions of the \"Orthodox\" continue to remain strong in the party, and, if necessary, we could use them to prevent the final turn of the party to the right. But do we need to interfere in the affairs of such a tiny state?";
								break;
							case 382:
								text2 = "Vita sine libertate, nihil!";
								text = " The ongoing protests in Italy are getting hotter and leading to more victims. On the outskirts of the country, the police almost completely lost control over the situation, not having time to catch looters and bandits who raised their heads. One of their victims was an elderly Japanese couple beaten to death by robbers after refusing to hand over an old watch. In the center of the country, the situation is not much better - in many cities, their own tent cities have appeared, trying to create a parallel government that strongly opposes the lawlessness of Andreotti and his cabinet. Paradoxically, local communal and local government services continue to perform their duties, having actually abandoned the ongoing clashes.";
								if (this.global1.data[175] == 1)
								{
									text += "| At this time, revolutionary sentiments spread to the small republic of San Marino, where people also take to the streets in support of Italian citizens and demand the resignation of local Christian Democrats. The local Communist Party is divided on the need to participate in the conflict, and it seems that only a small margin will be enough to swing the scales in one direction or another.";
								}
								text += "| The situation is getting more and more out of control, and a number of party members are offering to directly intervene in events in Italy in order to help the protesters succeed.";
								break;
							default:
								switch (number_event)
								{
								case 443:
									text2 = "Mongolian Perestroika";
									text = "The democratic processes that began in the USSR with the beginning of Perestroika almost immediately began to influence all countries dependent on the Land of Soviets. Mongolia, which has been effectively headed by Zhambyn Batmunkh since 1984 as the General Secretary of the Central Committee of the MPRP, was no exception. Due to growing economic problems, since 1986 the country was forced to begin cautious economic reforms to support personal and family cooperatives in agriculture, the number of which gradually began to increase. At the same time, state enterprises received greater financial autonomy, including through the abolition of subsidies to the most unprofitable industries. The reorganization of the main state economic institutions began. The number of administrative positions was reduced. In January 1988, at the Plenum of the Central Committee of the MPRP, the beginning of its own perestroika similar to the Soviet one was officially announced. It is possible that the gradual process would have continued further, but the famous chess player G. Kasparov recently gave an interview to an American publication that \"Mongolia can be sold to China to raise money for the Soviet Union\". These words had the effect of a bomb exploding among the democratic Mongolian opposition, which called for a further reduction in the influence of the USSR in the country and the acceleration of democratic reforms. The forces are consolidating around the \"young democrats\" in the persons of Sanjaasuren Zorig, Tsakhyagiin Elbegdorj, as well as Dari-Sukhbaatar and Chimediin Enkhbayar. Some of them are still counting on peaceful reforms and are waiting for the government's reaction. For our part, we could try to exert some influence on the course of Mongolian perestroika, if the affairs of distant Ulaanbaatar are of great importance to us.";
									break;
								case 444:
									text2 = "Upside-down board";
									text = "After the previous elections to the House of Representatives in July 1986, and especially since mid-1988, the ruling Liberal Democratic Party began to gradually lose its credibility among voters amid various scandals involving senior party officials and the introduction of an additional and highly unpopular sales tax. Despite the fact that after the new Prime Minister Toshiki Kaifu took office the ratings of the new Prime Minister began to rise, by the end of last year they began to fall again and by January they were already below 40%. Against this background, the LDP decided to be proactive and hold early elections, as demanded by the opposition. Many experts expect that this will be a confrontation between the LDP and the Socialist Party of Japan, which currently holds a leading position among all other parties due to its de facto control over the House of Councillors. ";
									if (this.global1.data[239] == 4)
									{
										text += "| The Communist Party is projected to be well on its way to repeating its '89 success and once again becoming the third force in the country. However, it is unlikely that it will be able to enter the government one way or another, given Kenji Miyamoto's stance on engagement with the JSP.";
									}
									else if (this.global1.data[239] == 6)
									{
										text += "| After the past investigations, the Communist Party is in a bad way, and a reformist faction led by Tetsuzo Fuwa is trying to take power. We could try to hold talks between the communists and the socialists, and perhaps, given the historicity of the moment, they can achieve more together.";
									}
									else if (this.global1.data[239] == 5)
									{
										text += "| Thanks to our support for Komeito, it is very likely that their position on cabinet formation will be decisive if the LDP fails to win a majority in the upcoming elections. Some of our analysts, who are sceptical about the possibility of the JSP coming to power and repeating last year's success in the House of Councillors elections, suggest informal talks between Komeito and the Liberal Democrats on the formation of a future coalition cabinet.";
									}
									else
									{
										text += "| The Socialist Party, despite the internal split it is experiencing, is currently experiencing a second breath, thanks to which the wind of change is beginning to blow. At the same time, the party is facing major financial problems that will force it to reduce the number of possible candidates to stand on behalf of the party in these elections.";
									}
									text = text + "| According to the information received from our agent, we could provide financial assistance to the party in these elections, or try to negotiate a new coalition with its allies across the political spectrum. We could also simply stay out of the elections and spend the money on more necessary purposes in our already difficult situation. Such a position is advocated by, for example, " + this.global1.politics_name[this.global1.data[13]] + ". The choice is in your hands.";
									break;
								case 445:
									text2 = "Rajiv's shadow";
									text = "The lower house of the Indian Lok Sabha parliament was prematurely dissolved by President of the Republic R. Venkataraman due to numerous governmental crises. In November 1990, the 11-month-old National Front coalition government led by Prime Minister V. P. Singh lost a vote of confidence in Parliament, and S. Shekhar of the Jamata Dal (Socialist) party was sworn in as the head of another minority government. On March 6, 1991, he in turn resigned over differences with former Prime Minister Rajiv Gandhi's INC (I), which led to the dissolution of the legislature a week later. The Indian National Congress's main opponents in the elections were still the conservative right-wing Bharatiya Jamata and the center-left National Front, which included the Jamata Dal and two communist parties. After the first day of voting on May 20, when 211 seats were filled, Gandhi was assassinated while campaigning near Madras. As a result, the remaining days of the election were postponed until mid-June. The final results did not give the party an absolute majority, although a marked shift to the right was visible on the first day. On June 21, new INC leader P.V. Narasimha Rao formed a minority government, which received parliamentary approval. For our part, we could reach out to the new government and discuss the future of relations between our countries in a changed world.";
									break;
								case 446:
									text2 = "The Miserables";
									text = "Against the backdrop of Monday demonstrations sweeping through the cities of the GDR, the Lusatian movement has unexpectedly revived—a cultural and national current demanding the restoration of its linguistic, educational, and traditional institutions. Its participants currently align with the broader protests, advocating for reforms and democratization, yet they increasingly emphasize their own cultural distinctiveness. In the corridors of power in the GDR, this is viewed with interest: some see in the movement an opportunity to strengthen internal stability by encouraging it as an example of loyal national consciousness; others suggest using Lusatian activism as a cover for the Stasi's operations abroad—connecting with foreign minority groups under the guise of \"friendship among small cultures\". There is also a third approach—to make the movement an internal tool: to pit the conservative Lusatian revival against the liberal-minded German dissidents, in order to divide and weaken the protest landscape. Whatever the choice may be, one thing is clear—the awakening of Lusatian identity has become a new factor in the political mosaic of the GDR, and now much depends on who can first channel this energy in the right direction.";
									break;
								case 447:
									text2 = "To the one who has grown old, youth will not return";
									text = "The swift campaign of Iraq against Kuwait, which took place last year and resulted in the near-total capitulation of Kuwaiti armed forces in just a few days, sparked widespread concern among Iraq's neighbouring republics. Consequently, in violation of agreements, American bases were established on Kuwaiti territory to deter any potential new Iraqi aggression. In response, Baghdad barred international observers from the remaining Kuwaiti northern lands under Iraqi control and annexed them to its Basra province, leading to the imposition of international sanctions against the Iraqi government. Thanks to the assets seized in Kuwait and the settlement of debts with its southern neighbour, Saddam Hussein has so far managed to maintain the previous standard of living in the country without any new reforms. Nevertheless, this situation cannot last forever, and against the backdrop of the rapid depletion of the Iraqi treasury and the transformation of international relations, Baghdad will be compelled to take new steps to strengthen its economic position. One manifestation of this continues to be the degradation of economic relations between Baghdad and Moscow due to the complications arising from the internal situation in the Soviet Union. Our country has longstanding ties with Ba'athist Iraq, which may compel Hussein to at least listen to our proposals for reforming Iraq's policies.";
									break;
								}
								break;
							}
						}
					}
					else if (number_event <= 1000)
					{
						switch (number_event)
						{
						case 900:
							text2 = "Clouds under the dome";
